 FINAL SOLUTION:
2025-12-01 10:15:55,737 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Below is a concise, evidence-backed guide to “where to start” with transformers/LLMs in healthcare: what works today, the kinds of datasets used, and core references on how these models work. I’ve grouped the most relevant and recent findings (with citations/links), and then listed widely used datasets and ready-to-use models you can build on.

What transformer/LLM models are and why they matter
- Core mechanics (how they work):
  - Attention Is All You Need (introduces the Transformer; encoder/decoder with self-attention; the basis for GPT/BERT-family and modern medical LLMs). Vaswani et al., 2017. https://huggingface.co/papers/1706.03762
  - Vision Transformer (ViT) applies transformers directly to image patches; strong transfer in medical imaging tasks when pre-trained at scale. Dosovitskiy et al., 2020. https://huggingface.co/papers/2010.11929
  - Swin Transformer (hierarchical, shifted windows; efficient multi-scale features for detection/segmentation). Liu et al., 2021. https://huggingface.co/papers/2103.14030

Recent clinical evidence that showcases effectiveness

Large language models (clinical knowledge, workflow, and RCTs)
- Health system–scale EHR LLM (NYUTron): Health system-scale language models are all-purpose prediction engines (Nature, 2023). Demonstrates an LLM trained on system-wide clinical notes can be adapted to a range of predictive tasks (e.g., readmission, mortality) within a single health system. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37286606/
- Med-PaLM 2 and MultiMedQA (Nature, 2023): “Large language models encode clinical knowledge.” Introduces MultiMedQA (6 medical QA datasets + HealthSearchQA) and a human evaluation framework (factuality, reasoning, harm). Shows strong clinical QA performance and sets a blueprint for rigorous, human-in-the-loop evaluation. DOI: 10.1038/s41586-023-06291-2; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37438534/
- Physician RCT with GPT-4 (Nature Medicine, 2025): 92 practicing physicians randomized. LLM assistance improved management reasoning scores vs usual resources by +6.5% (95% CI 2.7–10.2; P<0.001), with slightly longer time per case (+119 s). Validates benefit in complex, open-ended clinical reasoning—now needs real-world clinical validation. DOI: 10.1038/s41591-024-03456-y; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39910272/
- Ambient AI scribe randomized trials (2024–2025): 
  - Pediatric subspecialists randomized to Microsoft/Nuance DAX Copilot showed improved EHR experience (to 99th percentile) and reduced burnout; no significant change in after-hours “pajama time.” DOI: 10.1055/a-2657-8087; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40675605/
  - RCT comparing two ambient AI scribes (trial registered/preprint) assessing documentation efficiency and burnout (early randomized evidence base emerging). PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40672471/
- Specialty feasibility: Gynecologic cancer emergency care (2025): Compared GPT-4o vs a reasoning model (o3-mini-high) for emergency support feasibility. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41286925/
- Psychiatry EHR notes (non-English) outcome prediction (medRxiv, 2025): PsyRoBERTa (trained on ~44M psychiatry notes) improved acute psychiatric readmission prediction (AUC 0.74) and diagnosis recognition (AUC 0.85) vs baselines—underscores gains from domain-specific pretraining. medRxiv summary available via tools above.

Radiology and imaging (transformers and LLMs)
- Breast MRI BI-RADS from narrative reports using GPT-4o (2025): Automated BI-RADS assignment and management recommendations from free-text breast MRI reports lacking explicit BI-RADS. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40915935/
- Longitudinal MRI for Multiple Sclerosis disability (2024): A video ViT (ViViT) outperformed a CNN-LSTM for 6-year EDSS trinary prediction (AUC 0.84 vs 0.74; p<0.001), showing time-aware transformers can capture longitudinal imaging signals better at longer horizons. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38871944/
- Prospective patient communication RCT (radiology informed consent, late 2024): A RAG chatbot vs standard consultation for pre-CT consent achieved comparable comprehension and reduced physician consult time, though physicians were better at alleviating concerns. (Study summary from tool results.)

Ophthalmology and “oculomics” with foundation/transformer models
- Retinal fundus for diabetic kidney disease (DKD) triage (2025): Population-based study—deep learning on fundus images can detect DKD and distinguish diabetic nephropathy vs non-diabetic kidney disease, suggesting non-invasive “biopsy triage.” DOI: 10.1016/j.landig.2025.02.008; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40312169/
- Retinal fundus for carotid atherosclerosis (foundation models): DINOv2 + LoRA achieved AUROC 0.71, with prognostic relevance (HR 2.20 for CVD mortality); Grad-CAM explanations aligned with vessels—evidence that general vision FMs adapt well to retinal systemic risk tasks (summary via tool).
- Retinal imaging for stroke risk and SBI (DeepRETStroke; multi-country validation): High AUC for incident stroke (0.901) and recurrent stroke (0.769); prospective cohort showed added value vs clinical traits—illustrative of retinal FM transfer to systemic disease risk (summary via tool).
- Retinal age gap modeling with a foundation model associates with GGT (2025): PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40933305/

Systematic/umbrella reviews and real-world implementation
- Systematic review of real-world clinical LLM workflows (2025): Catalogs early implementations (documentation, decision support, communication) and barriers; calls for more prospective, standardized evaluation. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41098649/
- Umbrella review on ChatGPT in healthcare (2025): Broad applications with research gaps highlighted. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40335969/
- MRI productivity review (2025): 67 studies; AI improves workflow (scan acceleration, segmentation, triage), with some LLM emergence; emphasizes need for external validation and standardized metrics. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40361962/

Key public datasets used to build/evaluate healthcare transformers

EHR and clinical text
- MIMIC-III/IV (critical care EHRs; de-identified notes/labs/vitals): HF sample entries via tools (e.g., wshi83/EHRAgent-mimic_iii; TSaala/MIMICIVMortality).
- eICU Collaborative Research Database (ICU across sites): HF sample (wshi83/EHRAgent-eicu).
- HiRID (ICU; Switzerland): HF sample (Hiridharan10/clean_cf).
- i2b2/n2c2 shared tasks (de-id, cohort selection, ADE extraction): HF bigbio/n2c2_2018_track1 (trial eligibility) and bigbio/n2c2_2018_track2 (meds/ADEs).

Medical QA / reasoning benchmarks
- MedQA (USMLE) — HF: GBaker/MedQA-USMLE-4-options
- MedMCQA — HF: openlifescienceai/medmcqa
- PubMedQA — HF: qiaojin/PubMedQA
- MedNLI (clinical natural language inference) — HF: bigbio/mednli
- BioASQ (biomedical QA/retrieval) — HF: kroshan/BioASQ
- HealthSearchQA (consumer health queries; part of MultiMedQA) — HF: katielink/healthsearchqa

Radiology and medical imaging
- MIMIC-CXR (CXR images + reports) — HF: itsanmolgupta/mimic-cxr-dataset
- CheXpert (Stanford chest X-ray) — HF: danjacobellis/chexpert
- NIH ChestX-ray14 — HF: BahaaEldin0/NIH-Chest-Xray-14
- PadChest (Spanish chest X-ray) — HF: JasonZZ0601/PadChest-GR-Xray
- IU X-Ray (OpenI reports) — HF: fabnem/iu_xray; Dz-osamu/IU-Xray
- RSNA Pneumonia — HF: Baldezo313/rsna-pneumonia-dataset
- VinDr-CXR — HF: SampadKar/vindr-cxr
- Histopathology: CAMELYON16 — HF: owkin/camelyon16-features
- General medical imaging: MedMNIST — HF: albertvillanova/medmnist-v2

Ophthalmology (retina/OCT)
- EyePACS (diabetic retinopathy) — HF: bumbledeep/eyepacs
- APTOS 2019 (diabetic retinopathy) — HF: EslamHasan/APTOS2019DiabeticRetinopathy
- OCT 2017 — HF: Augusto777/OCT2017

Named entity recognition / de-id
- BC5CDR (chemical–disease NER; relations) — HF: bigbio/bc5cdr
- i2b2/Physionet de-id — HF: disi-unibo-nlp/physionet-deid-i2b2-2014

Ready-to-use medical/biomedical transformer models
- Bio_ClinicalBERT (clinical notes; widely used baseline) — HF: emilyalsentzer/Bio_ClinicalBERT
- GatorTron (8.9B parameters; >90B words including 82B clinical; improved NLI/MQA) — NPJ Digital Medicine, 2022. DOI: 10.1038/s41746-022-00742-2; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36572766/
- Meditron (LLaMA-2-based medical instruction-tuned; 7B/70B) — HF: epfl-llm/meditron-7b, -70b
- BioGPT (PubMed-pretrained generative model) — HF: microsoft/biogpt (and BioGPT-Large)
- Domain-specific variants (psychiatry/EHR, radiology instruction-tuned) are increasingly reported in the literature.

How to translate this evidence into your project (what to claim and how to back it)

- If you’re arguing “LLMs can improve clinician reasoning,” cite:
  - Nature Medicine RCT: +6.5% score improvement in management reasoning with GPT-4 assistance; DOI 10.1038/s41591-024-03456-y.
- If you’re arguing “LLMs reduce documentation burden,” cite:
  - Pediatric subspecialist RCT with DAX Copilot showing improved EHR experience and reduced burnout (no change in pajama time); DOI 10.1055/a-2657-8087.
- If you need “LLMs encode clinical knowledge at scale,” cite:
  - Med-PaLM 2/MultiMedQA (Nature 2023) with human evaluation on factuality, harm, bias; DOI 10.1038/s41586-023-06291-2. Also mention HealthSearchQA as a consumer health dataset within that benchmark.
- For “health-system EHR models generalize across tasks,” cite:
  - NYUTron (Nature 2023). PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37286606/
- For imaging transformers’ effectiveness:
  - ViViT > CNN-LSTM for long-horizon MS disability prediction (AUC 0.84) — PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38871944/
  - GPT-4o mapping free-text breast MRI reports to BI-RADS + management — PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40915935/
- For oculomics (retina as systemic biomarker):
  - DKD detection/differentiation via fundus (Lancet Digital Health, 2025) — DOI 10.1016/j.landig.2025.02.008.
  - Carotid atherosclerosis risk via DINOv2+LoRA (AUROC 0.71; prognostic hazard ratio 2.20) — dataset/model comparison study (summary above).

Suggested starting points (practical)
- Pick a use case and baseline:
  - EHR prediction (readmission/mortality): start with Bio_ClinicalBERT or Meditron; evaluate on MIMIC-III/IV and n2c2 track data; compare to logistic/CNN baselines.
  - Radiology report structuring: evaluate GPT-4o/Medtuned LLMs on MIMIC-CXR/CheXpert report fields; measure accuracy/F1 and “hallucinations” vs reference labels.
  - Patient-facing explanation/consent: prototype a RAG bot; measure comprehension, clarity, time saved; emulate the pre-CT informed consent study design.
  - Ophthalmology systemic risk: fine-tune a vision FM (DINOv2, ViT) with LoRA on EyePACS/APTOS for a surrogate risk task; report AUROC, calibration, and qualitative saliency alignment.
- Use established benchmarks for credibility:
  - Reasoning QA: MedQA, MedMCQA, PubMedQA, MedNLI, BioASQ, and MultiMedQA sets (HealthSearchQA) for general clinical knowledge/QA.
  - Imaging: MIMIC-CXR/CheXpert/ChestXray14 for chest imaging; CAMELYON16 for histopathology; include external validation if possible.
- Report with guardrails:
  - Human evaluation dimensions from MultiMedQA: factuality, reasoning, comprehension, possible harm, bias.
  - Safety: measure hallucinations, guideline-concordance (e.g., BI-RADS/TI-RADS), and fairness across subgroups.
  - Data privacy: prefer synthetic or de-identified notes; follow IRB and institutional policies.

Limitations to acknowledge
- Many studies are simulated or retrospective; prospective, real-world deployments with patient outcomes remain limited but are growing (e.g., documentation RCTs, physician RCT, prospective chatbot).
- Hallucinations, calibration, and bias remain concerns; human oversight and retrieval-grounding help.
- External validity and dataset shifts are common; multi-site validation is important.

If you need quick “anchor” citations in your write-up
- Transformers: Vaswani et al., 2017 (https://huggingface.co/papers/1706.03762)
- ViT: Dosovitskiy et al., 2020 (https://huggingface.co/papers/2010.11929)
- Swin: Liu et al., 2021 (https://huggingface.co/papers/2103.14030)
- NYUTron (Nature 2023): https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37286606/
- Med-PaLM 2 / MultiMedQA (Nature 2023): DOI 10.1038/s41586-023-06291-2
- GPT-4 RCT (Nature Medicine 2025): DOI 10.1038/s41591-024-03456-y
- Ambient scribe RCT (2025): DOI 10.1055/a-2657-8087
- GatorTron (NPJ Digital Medicine 2022): DOI 10.1038/s41746-022-00742-2
- MS long-horizon disability (ViViT vs CNN-LSTM, 2024): https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38871944
- Breast MRI BI-RADS via GPT-4o (2025): https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40915935
- DKD via fundus (2025): DOI 10.1016/j.landig.2025.02.008

This set should give you both the “how” (transformer mechanics), the “where they work today” (LLM, imaging, oculomics), and the “what to train/evaluate on” (datasets and benchmarks) to back your project with recent, real data.
