- Evaluating large language models for predicting psychiatric acute readmissions (PsyRoBERTa)
  - Developed PsyRoBERTa, the first psychiatry-specialized LLM trained on ~44 million Danish psychiatric-relevant clinical notes (population-based, eastern Denmark).
  - Compared against three public models (general or medical-domain pretrained) and a logistic regression baseline on predicting acute psychiatric readmissions; also tested psychiatric diagnosis recognition.
  - PsyRoBERTa outperformed prior models for readmission prediction (AUC = 0.74) and recognized psychiatric diagnoses well (AUC = 0.85).
  - Found the model captured features aligned with clinical practice; underscores importance of domain-specific pretraining for utilizing unstructured clinical notes, especially in non-English settings.

- Inferring pathogen superspreading potential from early spatial spread
  - Used a branching process model with US inter-county mobility; individual heterogeneity modeled via negative binomial secondary infections with mean R0 and dispersion r (lower r = more superspreading).
  - Stronger superspreading slowed early spatial invasion and increased variability across realizations.
  - A graph neural network inferred r reliably from early spatial patterns, robust to spatiotemporal R0 variation and underreporting.
  - Applied to early US COVID-19: strong superspreading pre-lockdown (r = 0.50, 95% CI 0.23–1.20) and weaker after (r = 1.3, 0.64–3.18).
