Tool `Paper Search:search_biorxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'vision transformer medical imaging radiology histopathology benchmark 2023 2024 2025', 'max_results': 15} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 8531 tokens): Here are concise summaries of the provided papers, preserving key methods, results, and implications:

- Programmed Delayed Splicing: A Mechanism for Timed Inflammatory Gene Expression (10.1101/443796; immunology)
  - Using hybrid capture of chromatin-associated, incompletely spliced pre-mRNAs and deep sequencing of NF-κB-regulated transcripts, the authors “freeze” transcription to measure subsequent splicing and find many introns splice tens to hundreds of times slower than average.
  - Conserved introns with weak splice donor sites act as timing elements; strengthening donor sequences by as few as two nucleotides in minigene assays markedly increases expression and mRNA production amplitude.
  - Deep learning with feature attribution identifies additional intronic and exonic motifs that contribute to delayed splicing independent of donor strength, supporting pre-mRNA splicing timing as a regulatory layer in inflammatory gene expression.

- Ddx3x regulates B-cell development and immunoglobulin rearrangement in mice (10.1101/452086; immunology)
  - Conditional Ddx3x deletion in developing B cells (Mb1-Cre; females) causes a block at the pro-B stage and complete loss of mature peripheral B cells; male hemizygous deletion (Vav1-Cre or Mb1-Cre) yields milder defects, suggesting partial compensation by Ddx3y or other male factors.
  - Loss of Ddx3x reduces Brwd1 expression, impairs proliferation control, disrupts Igκ rearrangement, and skews the BCR repertoire toward λ light chain.
  - Ddx3x is essential for coordinating proliferative and epigenetic programs needed for immunoglobulin gene rearrangement.

- Loss of ErbB3 redirects Integrin β1 from early endosomal recycling to secretion in extracellular vesicles (10.1101/575449; cell biology)
  - In breast epithelial cells, ErbB3 (without ligand-induced signaling) sorts internalized integrin β1 and transferrin receptor for recycling, likely via Rab4-positive endosomes.
  - ErbB3 depletion blocks recycling, redirects integrin β1 to lysosomal degradation and EV secretion, reduces surface integrin β1, impairs collective cell migration, and increases release of integrin β1-containing EVs that enhance motility of wild-type cells.
  - Mechanism: ErbB3 promotes assembly of the Arf6–GGA3–Rabaptin5 sorting complex to facilitate early recycling and suppress EV release, highlighting pseudo-RTK roles in trafficking.

- Supercentenarian and remarkable age records exhibit patterns indicative of clerical errors and pension fraud (10.1101/704080; developmental biology)
  - In the U.S., supercentenarian status is predicted by absence of vital registration; introduction of birth certificates coincides with a 69–82% drop in supercentenarian records.
  - In Italy, England, and France with uniform registration, remarkable longevity correlates with poverty, low income, high crime, worse health, shorter life expectancy, higher deprivation, and fewer 90+ year olds; old-age poverty explains >50% of regional variation in some countries.
  - Only 18% of exhaustively validated supercentenarians have birth certificates (0% in the U.S.); birthdates cluster on days divisible by five, suggesting widespread error/fraud. “Blue zones” align with poorer, lower-longevity regions, implying fraud/error as primary drivers of extreme age records.

- Oscillatory Multi-Timescale Mechanisms Underlying Audiovisual Sequence Prediction (10.1101/778969; neuroscience)
  - MEG during audiovisual serial prediction found theta-band (5–7 Hz) coupling linking cingulate, premotor, prefrontal, and superior temporal regions; behavioral performance varied with phase delays between nodes in this network.
  - Two beta-band power clusters (11–16 Hz and 16–22 Hz) increased when preceding or following items matched the current sequence element; these overlapped spatially with the theta network and showed cross-frequency coupling.
  - Results indicate multi-timescale neural dynamics with within- and cross-frequency coupling supporting multisenso
Tool `Paper Search:search_arxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'healthcare transformer EHR clinical outcome prediction 2023 2024 2025', 'max_results': 15} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 6339 tokens): - Video-R2: Reinforcing Consistent and Grounded Reasoning in Multimodal Language Models (Maaz et al.)
  - Diagnoses common video reasoning failures with two metrics: Think-Answer Consistency (TAC) for alignment between reasoning and answers, and Video Attention Score (VAS) for reliance on visual vs textual cues. Finds current models overuse linguistic priors across 11 benchmarks.
  - Proposes timestamp-aware supervised fine-tuning plus GRPO with a Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR) to enforce temporally aligned, causally coherent reasoning. Improves TAC, VAS, and accuracy consistently; code/data/models to be released.

- Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations (Rasheed et al.)
  - Introduces “Interactive Video Reasoning” where the model performs a Chain of Manipulations (re-watch, refocus, verify) to actively gather evidence, overcoming passive, text-only reasoning.
  - Trains on Video-CoM Instruct (18K) and refines with GRPO using step-level reasoning rewards. Achieves +3.6% avg over recent SOTA across 9 benchmarks using only 25K SFT and 3K RL samples; code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM

- Thinking by Doing: Efficient World Model Reasoning via Multi-turn Interaction (Shu et al.)
  - WMAct removes rigid reasoning templates, enabling active learning through interaction. Two mechanisms: reward rescaling (incentivize efficient, non-redundant actions) and interaction frequency annealing (gradually reduce allowed turns to internalize dynamics).
  - On Sokoban, Maze, Taxi, solves tasks in single turns that previously needed multiple, with strong transfer to harder settings.

- AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (Zhong et al.)
  - Extends Diffusion Transformer with identity-aware attention to iteratively process identity–audio pairs, enabling arbitrary scaling of drivable identities.
  - Trains primarily on single-person videos and refines with few multi-person clips; proposes metric and dataset for interactivity. Delivers strong lip sync, visual quality, and natural multi-person interactivity.

- A Lyapunov-Based Small-Gain Theorem for Fixed-Time Stability (Tang, Krstic, Poveda)
  - Establishes a Lyapunov-based nonlinear small-gain condition ensuring fixed-time stability (FxTS) of interconnected systems when each subsystem admits a fixed-time ISS Lyapunov function.
  - Fills a gap versus asymptotic and finite-time results; illustrated analytically and numerically, including fixed-time feedback optimization without time-scale separation.

- ThetaEvolve: Test-time Learning on Open Problems (Wang et al.)
  - Open-source framework that unifies in-context learning and RL at test time for evolving programs to improve bounds on open optimization problems.
  - Uses a single LLM, a large program database, batch sampling, lazy penalties, and optional reward shaping. Enables a small open-source model (DeepSeek-R1-0528-Qwen3-8B) to set new best-known bounds (e.g., circle packing, first auto-correlation inequality) and shows RL checkpoints generalize faster and better. Code: https://github.com/ypwang61/ThetaEvolve

- RapunSL: Untangling Quantum Computing with Separation, Linear Combination and Mixing (Matsushita et al.)
  - Proposes a quantum separation logic supporting basis-locality and outcome-locality, reducing reasoning about superpositions and mixed states to pure states.
  - Introduces new connectives (linear combination, mixing) alongside separation, dramatically improving scalability on challenging case studies.

- Wilson loops, symmetries, and selective bulk-boundary correspondence in HOTIs (Aich, Seradjeh)
  - Studies chiral-symmetric Bloch Hamiltonians generalizing the π-flux quadrupole model, with separable and nonseparable cases. Product of subsystem chiral windings works only for separable systems.
  - Introduces gauge-independent mirror-filtered winding numbers for Wannier Hamiltonians and periodicized Wilson-line invariants tied to Wannier gap
