- GPT-4 assistance for improvement of physician performance on patient care tasks: a randomized controlled trial (Goh E et al.; doi: 10.1038/s41591-024-03456-y; Pub date: 2025-01-01; https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39910272/)
  - Question: Does LLM assistance (GPT-4) improve physician management reasoning on open-ended clinical tasks versus conventional resources?
  - Design: Prospective, randomized, controlled trial (Nov 2023–Apr 2024); 92 practicing physicians; five expert-developed vignettes based on real, de-identified cases with sequential information disclosure to simulate clinical workflow.
  - Arms: GPT-4 plus conventional resources vs conventional resources alone. Also compared LLM-augmented physicians with LLM-alone performance.
  - Outcomes:
    - Primary: Total score on expert-developed scoring rubric.
    - Secondary: Domain-specific scores; time spent per case.
  - Results:
    - Performance: LLM-assisted physicians scored higher than controls (mean difference 6.5%, 95% CI 2.7 to 10.2; P < 0.001).
    - Time: LLM users spent more time per case (+119.3 seconds, 95% CI 17.4 to 221.2; P = 0.02).
    - LLM-augmented vs LLM alone: No significant difference in scores (-0.9%, 95% CI -9.0 to 7.2; P = 0.8).
  - Interpretation: GPT-4 assistance can improve physician management reasoning in simulated complex cases but may increase time spent; parity with LLM-alone suggests physicians did not exceed model performance on scoring metrics. Validation in real clinical settings is needed.
  - Registration: ClinicalTrials.gov NCT06208423.

