  - Aim: Build the first psychiatry-specialized LLM on non-English EHR notes and evaluate prediction of acute psychiatric readmissions.
  - Data/Model: ~44 million psychiatry-relevant clinical notes from eastern Denmark; compared with three public general/medical-domain models and a logistic regression baseline.
  - Results: PsyRoBERTa outperformed prior models for readmission prediction (AUC=0.74) and recognized psychiatric diagnoses (AUC=0.85); captured clinically aligned features.
  - Implications: Domain-specific pretraining materially improves performance; demonstrates LLMs’ potential for leveraging psychiatric notes.

- Inferring pathogen superspreading potential using early spatial spread patterns
  - Aim: Quantify superspreading from early spatial invasion using population-level data.
  - Methods: US county-level branching process with inter-county mobility; negative binomial transmission with mean R0 and dispersion r; graph neural network infers r from spatial spread; robust to R0 heterogeneity and underreporting.
  - Results: Stronger superspreading slows early spatial spread and increases realization variability. Early US COVID-19: pre-lockdown r=0.50 (95% CI 0.23–1.20) → post-lockdown r=1.3 (0.64–3.18).
  - Implications: Early spatial patterns can reliably estimate superspreading potential.

- Impact of later trading hours for bars/clubs on alcohol-related harms in Scotland: a controlled interrupted time series
  - Setting: Aberdeen (38 bars extended 1–3 hours to 3am), Glasgow (10 clubs extended 1 hour to 4am), 2015–2021; Edinburgh as control.
  - Outcomes: Weekend night alcohol-related ambulance call-outs (primary), crimes (secondary).
  - Results: Aberdeen saw a 17.4% relative increase in call-outs (effect size 7.061; 95% CI 3.902–10.220; p<0.001) and 7.9% increase in crimes (effect size 3.187; 95% CI 0.564–5.810; p=0.017; not robust in sensitivity analyses). Glasgow effects were not significant/robust.
  - Implications: Later hours increase harms where more/larger premises extend; local context (number, capacity, type) moderates effects; informs licensing policy.

- Sex differences in the protective effect of brain volume after ischemic stroke
  - Cohort: 1,103 AIS patients (48% female), 2015–2024. Outcomes: 90-day functional independence (mRS 0–2).
  - Measures: Parenchymal brain volume (PBV) from CT; final infarct volume (DWI-FIV) from 24–48h MRI.
  - Results: Independence: 51.7% males vs 44.9% females (p=0.025). PBV protective in univariable for both sexes; in multivariable, protective in males (aOR 1.30 per 100 cm3; 95% CI 1.10–1.60) but not females (aOR 1.10; 0.90–1.33). Each decade reduced independence odds by 30% in females (aOR 0.70; 0.62–0.79) vs 15% in males (aOR 0.85; 0.72–0.97; interaction p=0.019). Age increased infarct volume in females (+4.22 cm3/decade; p=0.033) but not males.
  - Implications: Aging diminishes brain reserve benefits more in women; incorporate sex and brain volume in outcome models.

- Concordance between IDSA UTI guidelines and ED practice
  - Design: 15-month review of 50 adult ED patients with urinalysis (excluding sepsis/empiric antibiotic needs) at Long Island Jewish Medical Center.
