- Evaluating large language models for predicting psychiatric acute readmissions (PsyRoBERTa)
  - Built PsyRoBERTa, the first psychiatry-specialized LLM trained on ~44 million clinical notes from eastern Denmark.
  - Compared against three public general/medical models and a logistic regression baseline for predicting acute psychiatric readmissions; also evaluated diagnosis recognition.
  - Outperformed prior models: AUC=0.74 for readmission; AUC=0.85 for recognizing psychiatric diagnoses.
  - Findings highlight the value of domain-specific pretraining and show model features align with clinical practice; addresses non-English, psychiatry-specific data gaps.

- Inferring pathogen superspreading potential from early spatial spread
  - Branching-process model with inter-county mobility in the US; secondary infections follow negative binomial with mean R0 and dispersion r (superspreading).
  - Stronger superspreading slows early spatial invasion and increases variability across realizations.
  - A graph neural network infers r from early spatial patterns, robust to spatiotemporal R0 variation and underreporting.
  - Early US COVID-19: strong superspreading pre-lockdown (r=0.50, 95% CI 0.23–1.20), weaker post-lockdown (r=1.3, 0.64–3.18).
  - Offers a population-level approach to quantify superspreading without cluster/genomic data.

- Later trading hours and alcohol-related harms in Scotland
  - Controlled interrupted time series (2015–2021) of local policy changes: 38 Aberdeen bars extended 1–3 hours (to 3am); 10 Glasgow nightclubs extended 1 hour (to 4am).
  - Primary outcome: weekend night alcohol-related ambulance call-outs; secondary: crimes.
  - Aberdeen: significant increases vs Edinburgh control—ambulance call-outs +17.4% (effect size 7.061; 95% CI 3.902–10.220; p<0.001); crimes +7.9% (effect size 3.187; 0.564–5.810; p=0.017; not robust in sensitivity).
  - Glasgow: no significant, robust effects.
  - Suggests harms depend on number/capacity/type of premises; informs licensing policy design.

- Sex differences in brain volume protection after ischemic stroke
  - Multicenter prospective registry (n=1,103; 48% female). Automated parenchymal brain volume (PBV) and 24–48h DWI final infarct volume (FIV).
  - 90-day independence: 51.7% males vs 44.9% females (p=0.025).
  - PBV associated with independence in both sexes univariably, but multivariable: males aOR 1.30 per 100 cm3 (1.10–1.60); females aOR 1.10 (0.90–1.33).
  - Each decade of age reduced independence odds by 30% in females (aOR 0.70; 0.62–0.79) vs 15% in males (aOR 0.85; 0.72–0.97); interaction p=0.019.
  - Age linked to larger infarcts in females (+4.22 cm3/decade; p=0.033). Aging attenuates brain reserve in women; include sex and PBV in models.

- ED practice vs IDSA guidance for UTI testing/treatment
  - 15-month review of 50 adults with urinalysis (UA) in an urban ED; excluded sepsis/empiric antibiotics cases.
  - 64% asymptomatic; none met IDSA criteria for UA testing.
  - Symptomatic vs asymptomatic: positive UA 72.2% vs 43.8% (p=0.06); antibiotics for positive UA 61.5% vs 14.3% (p=0.0128); antibiotics for negative UA 20.0% vs 0% (p=0.05).
  - Significant discordance with IDSA; highlights need for education to reduce unnecessary UA/antibiotics and associated harms.

- Mean corpuscular volume in HFE C282Y/H63D compound heterozygotes
  - Retrospective study of 74 non-Hispanic white adults with high iron phenotypes; excluded anemia, pregnancy, MCV-raising meds.
  - Mean MCV 95.7±4.0 fL.
  - MCV correlated negatively with BMI (p=0.0488) and positively with age (p=0.0098), daily heme iron (p=0.0333), and alcohol intake (p=0.0113).
  - Heavy alcohol: MCV 97.8±3.8 vs 95.0±3.9 fL (p=0.0074). Regression confirmed associations with age (p=0.0064) and alcohol (p=0.0151).
  - No significant associations with sex, diabetes, non-heme/supplemental iron, MCP joints, TS, or SF.


