- Programmed Delayed Splicing: A Mechanism for Timed Inflammatory Gene Expression (doi:10.1101/443796; 2025-11-24; immunology)
  - Approach: Hybrid capture of chromatin-associated pre-mRNAs, deep sequencing; transcriptional “freeze” to observe splicing kinetics; minigene reporters; deep learning feature attribution.
  - Findings: Many introns in the NF-κB transcriptome splice tens to hundreds of times slower than average. Weak, conserved donor sites act as timing elements; strengthening donors by as few as two nucleotides markedly increases expression and shortens delay. Models identify additional intronic/exonic motifs linked to slow splicing.
  - Implication: Programmed splicing delays tune onset and amplitude of inflammatory gene expression; likely generalizable to other rapid-response programs.

- Ddx3x regulates B-cell development and immunoglobulin rearrangement in mice (doi:10.1101/452086; 2025-11-28; immunology)
  - Approach: Conditional Ddx3x loss (Mb1-Cre, Vav1-Cre) in mice; developmental and repertoire analyses.
  - Findings: Female homozygous deletion causes complete absence of mature B cells with a block at pro-B stage. Male hemizygous loss shows milder deficits and marginal zone skew, suggesting partial Ddx3y compensation. Mechanism: reduced Brwd1, failure to curtail proliferation, defective Igk rearrangement; lambda light chain skew.
  - Implication: Ddx3x is essential for B-cell development via epigenetic and cell-cycle control of Ig rearrangement.

- Loss of ErbB3 redirects Integrin β1 from early endosomal recycling to secretion in extracellular vesicles (doi:10.1101/575449; 2025-11-13; cell biology)
  - Approach: Breast epithelial models; trafficking assays; EV analysis.
  - Findings: ErbB3 sorts internalized integrin β1 and TfR for Rab4-positive recycling independently of ligand signaling. ErbB3 loss redirects integrin β1 to lysosomes and EV secretion, reducing surface integrin and impairing collective migration. Secreted EVs from ErbB3-depleted cells enhance motility of wild-type cells. Mechanism: assembly of Arf6–GGA3–Rabaptin5 complex.
  - Implication: Pseudo-RTKs actively regulate endosomal sorting and EV release.

