Tool `Paper Search:search_arxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'RETFound retinal foundation model Nature 2023', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 4699 tokens): Here’s a concise synthesis of the provided papers, capturing key problems, methods, and results:

- Video-R2: Reinforcing Consistent and Grounded Reasoning in Multimodal Language Models (cs.CV)
  - Problem: Video reasoning in MLLMs often appears convincing but is logically inconsistent and weakly grounded in visual evidence.
  - Diagnostics: Proposes Think Answer Consistency (TAC) to measure reasoning–answer alignment, and Video Attention Score (VAS) to quantify reliance on visual vs textual cues. Analysis across 11 benchmarks shows heavy reliance on linguistic priors.
  - Method: Two-stage post-training—timestamp-aware supervised fine-tuning + GRPO with a Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR)—to improve temporal precision and causal coherence.
  - Results: Consistent gains in TAC, VAS, and accuracy across multiple video reasoning benchmarks; promises code/dataset/model open-sourcing.

- Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations (cs.CV)
  - Problem: Most MLLMs “think about videos” passively after a single encoding; cannot rewatch/refocus/verify evidence.
  - Paradigm: Interactive Video Reasoning—models “think with videos” by performing iterative visual actions.
  - Model: Video-CoM reasons via Chain of Manipulations (CoM).
  - Data/RL: Video-CoM Instruct (18K instruction-tuning examples). Reinforcement learning with reasoning-aware GRPO, introducing step-level reasoning rewards (beyond sparse answer rewards) to improve grounding and consistency.
  - Efficiency/Results: Trained on only 25K SFT + 3K GRPO video samples; improves average performance by 3.6% over recent SOTA across 9 video reasoning benchmarks. Ablations show gains in accuracy and interpretability.
  - Code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM

- Thinking by Doing (WMAct): Building Efficient World Model Reasoning in LLMs via Multi-turn Interaction (cs.AI)
  - Problem: Rigid, structured reasoning during interaction hinders active learning and efficient world-model internalization.
  - Approach: WMAct enables models to shape thinking through doing via:
    - Reward rescaling based on action efficacy to reduce redundancy and encourage purposeful interaction.
    - Interaction frequency annealing to progressively reduce allowed turns, pushing internalization of dynamics and less reliance on external feedback.
  - Results: On Sokoban, Maze, and Taxi, WMAct develops world model reasoning allowing single-turn solutions formerly requiring multiple interactions; demonstrates strong transfer to more complex environments and broader reasoning benchmarks.

- AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (cs.CV)
