Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'vision transformer histopathology cancer benchmark 2023 2024', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result: [{"paper_id":"38871944","title":"Time-Dependent Deep Learning Prediction of Multiple Sclerosis Disability.","authors":"Mayfield JD; Murtagh R; Ciotti J; Robertson D; Naqa IE","abstract":"The majority of deep learning models in medical image analysis concentrate on single snapshot timepoint circumstances, such as the identification of current pathology on a given image or volume. This is often in contrast to the diagnostic methodology in radiology where presumed pathologic findings are correlated to prior studies and subsequent changes over time. For multiple sclerosis (MS), the current body of literature describes various forms of lesion segmentation with few studies analyzing disability progression over time. For the purpose of longitudinal time-dependent analysis, we propose a combinatorial analysis of a video vision transformer (ViViT) benchmarked against traditional recurrent neural network of Convolutional Neural Network-Long Short-Term Memory (CNN-LSTM) architectures and a hybrid Vision Transformer-LSTM (ViT-LSTM) to predict long-term disability based upon the Extended Disability Severity Score (EDSS). The patient cohort was procured from a two-site institution with 703 patients' multisequence, contrast-enhanced MRIs of the cervical spine between the years 2002 and 2023. Following a competitive performance analysis, a VGG-16-based CNN-LSTM was compared to ViViT with an ablation analysis to determine time-dependency of the models. The VGG16-LSTM predicted trinary classification of EDSS score in 6 years with 0.74 AUC versus the ViViT with 0.84 AUC (p-value < 0.001 per 5 × 2 cross-validation F-test) on an 80:20 hold-out testing split. However, the VGG16-LSTM outperformed ViViT when patients with only 2 years of MRIs (n = 94) (0.75 AUC versus 0.72 AUC, respectively). Exact EDSS classification was investigated for both models using both classification and regression strategies but showed collectively worse performance. Our experimental results demonstrate the ability of time-dependent deep learning models to predict disability in MS using trinary stratification of disability, mimicking clinical practice. Further work includes external validation and subsequent observational clinical trials.","doi":"10.1007/s10278-024-01031-y","published_date":"2024-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38871944/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2024-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""}]
Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'radiology large language model report generation evaluation 2023 2024', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 2787 tokens): Here is a concise synthesis of the provided studies, preserving key objectives, methods, models, datasets, metrics, and main findings.

Overall theme
- Focus areas: post-deployment AI monitoring, privacy-preserving structured report generation, guideline-based follow-up recommendations, multimodal/image understanding, adherence to LLM reporting standards, and EHR applications.
- Modalities and tasks: radiology report structuring, lymphoma progression assessment, pancreatic cancer and cystic lesion reporting, pulmonary nodule follow-up, thyroid ultrasound TI-RADS classification, and radiologic impression generation.

Study summaries
1) Post-deployment Monitoring of AI Performance in Intracranial Hemorrhage Detection by ChatGPT (Aidoc; 2025-01-01; doi:10.1016/j.acra.2025.07.055)
- Objective: Evaluate post-deployment performance of a commercial AI (Aidoc) for ICH detection; assess ChatGPT-4 Turbo for automated monitoring.
- Notes: Concept demonstrates using an LLM to monitor deployed imaging AI. Specific performance metrics not provided in abstract.

2) Privacy-Preserving LLMs for Structured Lymphoma Progression Reports (Llama 3.3 vs Llama 4; 2025-01-01; doi:10.1007/s10278-025-01618-z)
- Design: Single-center, retrospective (July 2023–July 2024), n=65 adult lymphoma patients. Locally deployed Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct and Llama-4-Scout-17B-16E-Instruct with chain-of-thought prompting across three iterations. Two radiologists scored nodal/extranodal involvement, Lugano stage, and treatment response.
- Metrics: Per-label F1, accuracy, recall, precision, specificity; case-weighted averages with 95% CIs.
- Results:
  - Template structure correctly implemented by both models for all cases.
  - Extraction accuracy: Llama-4-Scout higher for nodal involvement (0.99 [0.98–0.99] vs 0.97 [0.95–0.96], p<0.001) and extranodal (0.99 [0.99–1.00] vs 0.99 [0.98–0.99], p=0.013).
  - Clinical inferences: Larger gaps for Lugano stage (0.85 [0.79–0.89] vs 0.60 [0.53–0.67], p<0.001) and treatment response (0.88 [0.83–0.92] vs 0.65 [0.58–0.71], p<0.001).
  - No hallucinations of new disease sites. Highest relative error in interpreting post-treatment disease level.
- Conclusion: Privacy-preserving LLMs excel at information extraction and templating but are less reliable for generating new clinical inferences; useful for streamlining documentation with areas needing optimization.

3) LLMs for Pulmonary Nodule Follow-up Recommendations (2025-01-01; doi:10.1016/j.ejro.2025.100655)
- Objective: Assess LLMs’ ability to generate guideline-concordant follow-up recommendations leveraging radiology findings and management guidelines.
- Details: Specific models/metrics not provided in abstract.

4) GPT-4 for Converting Mixed-Language CT Reports (PDAC) to NCCN Structured Templates and Categorizing Resectability (2025-01-01; doi:10.3348/kjr.2024.1228)
- Objective: Evaluate GPT-4 for transforming English/Korean narrative CT reports for PDAC into NCCN SRs and accurately categorizing resectability.
- Details: Feasibility/accuracy explored; metrics not included in abstract.

