Tool `Paper Search:search_medrxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'Articulate Medical Intelligence Explorer AMIE clinical conversations 2024', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 6884 tokens): Summaries of included papers

1) Evaluating large language models for predicting psychiatric acute readmissions from clinical notes of population-based EHR (PsyRoBERTa)
- Problem: Lack of psychiatry- and non-English–specialized LLMs limits use of clinical notes.
- Data/Model: PsyRoBERTa pretrained on ~44M psychiatric-relevant clinical notes from eastern Denmark; compared with 3 public general/medical-domain models and a logistic regression baseline.
- Task/Results: Predicting psychiatric acute readmissions (AUC=0.74, best among comparators). Also recognized psychiatric diagnoses (AUC=0.85).
- Conclusion: Domain-specific pretraining materially improves performance; LLMs can leverage psychiatry notes to predict outcomes.

2) Inferring pathogen superspreading potential using early spatial spread patterns
- Problem: Estimating superspreading (dispersion r) often needs biased/limited tracing or genomics.
- Methods: Branching process with inter-county US mobility; negative binomial offspring with R0 and dispersion r; graph neural network (GNN) infers r from early spatial spread; robust to R0 variation and underreporting.
- Findings: Stronger superspreading slows early spatial invasion and increases outcome variability. Early US COVID-19: strong superspreading pre-lockdown (r=0.50, 95% CI 0.23–1.20); weaker post-lockdown (r=1.3, 0.64–3.18).
- Conclusion: Population-level geographic spread can quantify superspreading.

3) Impact of later trading hours for bars/clubs on alcohol-related ambulance call-outs and crimes in Scotland
- Design: Controlled interrupted time series, 2015–2021. Aberdeen (38 bars extended 1–3 h to 3am), Glasgow (10 nightclubs +1 h to 4am). Control: Edinburgh.
- Outcomes: Weekend nighttime alcohol-related ambulance call-outs (primary); crimes (secondary).
- Results: Aberdeen saw a 17.4% relative increase in ambulance call-outs (p<0.001); crimes +7.9% (p=0.017) but not robust in sensitivity analyses. No significant, robust effects in Glasgow.
- Implications: Harms depend on number/capacity/type of premises; informs nuanced licensing policies.

4) Sex differences in protective effect of brain volume after acute ischemic stroke
- Cohort: 1,103 AIS patients (48% female), median age 66; automated PBV (non-contrast CT) and DWI-FIV (MRI 24–48h). Outcome: 90-day mRS 0–2.
- Results: Functional independence: 51.7% males vs 44.9% females (p=0.025). Univariable: higher PBV protective in both sexes. Multivariable: PBV remains protective in males (aOR 1.30 per 100 cm3; 95% CI 1.10–1.60) but not females (aOR 1.10; 0.90–1.33). Each decade of age reduces independence more in females (aOR 0.70; 0.62–0.79) than males (0.85; 0.72–0.97; interaction p=0.019). Age associates with larger infarcts in females (+4.22 cm3/decade; p=0.033) not males.
- Conclusion: Aging attenuates brain-volume protection in women; incorporate sex and PBV in prognostic models.

5) Concordance between IDSA UTI testing/treating criteria vs ED practice
- Design: 15-month review, 50 adults with UA at a US urban ED; excluded sepsis/empiric antibiotics.
- Findings: 64% were asymptomatic; none met IDSA criteria for UA testing. Symptomatic patients had more positive UAs (72.2% vs 43.8%, p=0.06) and were more often given antibiotics for positive UA (61.5% vs 14.3%, p=0.0128) and for negative UA (20.0% vs 0%, p=0.05).
- Conclusion: Significant over-testing/treatment of ASB; supports educational interventions to reduce inappropriate UAs/antibiotics and associated harms (resistance, adverse events, C. difficile).

6) Mean corpuscular volume in HFE p.C282Y/p.H63D compound heterozygotes with high iron phenotypes
- Sample: 74 non-Hispanic white adults (37 men, 37 women), mean age 59±12, elevated TS/SF; excluded anemia/pregnancy/MCV-raising meds.
