Tool `Paper Search:search_arxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'CheXzero self-supervised chest radiograph Nature Medicine 2022', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 5242 tokens): - AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (arXiv:2511.23475v1, cs.CV, 2025-11-28)
  - Zhong et al. propose AnyTalker, a multi-person audio-driven talking video framework with an extensible multi-stream architecture.
  - Key idea: a Diffusion Transformer extended with identity-aware attention that iteratively processes identity–audio pairs, enabling arbitrary scaling to many identities.
  - Training pipeline learns multi-person speaking/interactivity patterns using only single-person videos, with minimal real multi-person clips for refinement.
  - Contributes a metric and dataset targeting naturalness and interactivity in multi-person talking videos.
  - Results: strong lip-sync, visual quality, and coherent interactivity; balances data cost and identity scalability.

- Convergence and front position for an FKPP-type free boundary problem (arXiv:2511.23457v1, math.AP/math.PR, 2025-11-28)
  - Berestycki, Penington, Tough analyze a free boundary PDE arising as the hydrodynamic limit of N-BBM, long conjectured in the FKPP universality class.
  - Prove convergence to the minimal traveling wave for initial conditions decaying sufficiently fast at +∞.
  - Precisely link initial tail decay to the free boundary position L_t with Bramson-type asymptotics: L_t = sqrt(2) t − (3/(2 sqrt 2)) log t + c + o(1), with necessary and sufficient conditions.
  - Extend to a β-parameterized problem showing transitions from pulled to pushed and a critical “pushmi-pullyu” regime, giving sharp necessary/sufficient conditions and front-position asymptotics in all regimes.

- From metallicity distributions to mutual information: A new perspective on stellar halo assembly (arXiv:2511.23452v1, astro-ph.GA/CO, 2025-11-28)
  - Mondal & Pandey use five Aquarius Milky Way-mass halos to introduce mutual information (MI) between angular position and metallicity as an information-theoretic diagnostic.
  - MDFs vary from single-peaked (few massive progenitors) to broad/bimodal (multiple accretions), reflecting stochastic assembly.
  - Low-metallicity stars show stronger spatial anisotropy and angular clustering; removing bound satellites reduces anisotropy, but high-metallicity stars remain marginally more anisotropic.
  - MI rises with radius then saturates; deviations from randomized controls are concentrated in the inner halo, indicating residual spatial–chemical coupling from incomplete mixing.
  - Framework provides an intuitive, quantitative way to compare simulations with high-dimensional surveys.

- Towards Continuous Intelligence Growth: Self-Training, Continual Learning, and Dual-Scale Memory in SuperIntelliAgent (arXiv:2511.23436v1, cs.AI, 2025-11-28)
  - Lin et al. introduce SuperIntelliAgent: a trainable small diffusion model (learner) paired with a frozen LLM (verifier) to generate self-supervised DPO preference pairs during inference.
  - Dual-scale memory: short-term in-context memory preserves reasoning traces across refinement; long-term memory performs lightweight on-the-fly fine-tuning.
  - Replay buffer keeps samples showing verifiable progress for adaptive curricula and continual improvement.
  - Infrastructure-agnostic; turns ordinary inference loops into lifelong optimization. With few auto-generated DPO pairs, the learner improves across benchmarks.

- COMPAS: A Distributed Multi-Party SWAP Test for Parallel Quantum Algorithms (arXiv:2511.23434v1, quant-ph, 2025-11-28)
  - Goldstein-Gelb et al. present COMPAS, an architecture for multivariate trace estimation across modular, distributed QPUs using pre-shared Bell pairs.
  - Achieves constant depth overhead and Bell-pair consumption linear in circuit width; suitable for near-term hardware.
  - Unlike existing schemes trading off circuit depth vs GHZ width, COMPAS attains asymptotic optimality in both simultaneously.
  - Analyze network-level errors and simulate circuit-level noise. Applicable to Rényi entropy estimation, virtual cooling/distill
