Tool `Paper Search:search_medrxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'conversational diagnostic AI AMIE 2024', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 6884 tokens): - Evaluating large language models for predicting psychiatric acute readmissions from clinical notes of population-based EHR (PsyRoBERTa)
  - Built the first psychiatry-specialized LLM trained on ~44 million psychiatric-relevant clinical notes from eastern Denmark.
  - Compared to three public models (general- or medical-domain pretrained) and a logistic regression baseline.
  - Predicting acute psychiatric readmissions: AUC=0.74 (outperformed prior models); psychiatric diagnosis recognition: AUC=0.85.
  - Highlights the value of domain-specific pretraining for leveraging unstructured notes in psychiatry.

- Inferring pathogen superspreading potential using early spatial spread patterns
  - Branching process model with US inter-county mobility; transmission heterogeneity via negative binomial with R0 and dispersion r.
  - Stronger superspreading (lower r) slows early spatial invasion and increases variability between realizations.
  - A graph neural network infers r from early spatial spread, robust to spatiotemporal R0 variation and underreporting.
  - Early US COVID-19: strong superspreading pre-lockdown r=0.50 (95% CI: 0.23–1.20); weaker post-lockdown r=1.3 (0.64–3.18).
  - Provides a population-level method to quantify superspreading.

- Impact of later trading hours for bars/clubs on alcohol-related harms in Scotland: controlled interrupted time series
  - Natural experiment (2015–2021): Aberdeen (38 bars extended 1–3 hours up to 3am); Glasgow (10 nightclubs extended 1 hour to 4am). Control: Edinburgh.
  - Outcomes: weekend nighttime alcohol-related ambulance call-outs (primary) and crimes (secondary).
  - Aberdeen: call-outs increased 17.4% (effect size 7.061; 95% CI 3.902–10.220; p<0.001); crimes +7.9% (effect size 3.187; 95% CI 0.564–5.810; p=0.017; not robust in sensitivity analyses).
  - Glasgow: no significant, robust changes.
  - Effects moderated by number/capacity/type of premises; implications for local/national licensing policy.

- Sex Differences in the Protective Effect of Brain Volume after AIS
  - Multicenter AIS registry (2015–2024), N=1,103; 48% female; median age 66; median NIHSS 9; median DWI-FIV 7.8 cm3.
  - 90-day functional independence (mRS 0–2): 51.7% males vs 44.9% females (p=0.025).
  - PBV association with independence: males aOR 1.30 per 100 cm3 (95% CI 1.10–1.60); females aOR 1.10 (0.90–1.33).
  - Each decade of age reduced independence: females aOR 0.70 (0.62–0.79) vs males 0.85 (0.72–0.97); interaction p=0.019.
  - Age linked to larger infarcts in females (+4.22 cm3/decade; p=0.033), not in males. Aging attenuates brain reserve in women.

- Concordance between IDSA criteria for UTI testing/treatment vs ED practice
  - 15-month review of 50 adult ED patients with urinalysis; excluded sepsis/empiric antibiotic indications.
  - 64% asymptomatic; none met IDSA criteria for UA testing.
  - Symptomatic vs asymptomatic: positive UA 72.2% vs 43.8% (p=0.06); antibiotics given for positive UA 61.5% vs 14.3% (p=0.0128); antibiotics for negative UA 20.0% vs 0% (p=0.05).
  - Significant guideline discordance; underscores need for education to reduce unnecessary testing and antibiotics.

- Mean corpuscular volume in HFE p.C282Y/p.H63D compound heterozygotes with high iron
  - Retrospective study of 74 non-Hispanic white adults with high iron phenotypes; mean age 59±12; mean MCV 95.7±4.0 fL.
  - MCV correlated negatively with BMI (p=0.0488) and positively with age (p=0.0098), daily heme iron (p=0.0333), and alcohol intake (p=0.0113).
  - Heavy alcohol intake associated with higher MCV: 97.8±3.8 vs 95.0±3.9 fL (p=0.0074).
  - Regression confirmed positive associations with age (p=0.0064) and alcohol (p=0.0151); no links with sex, diabetes, non-heme/supplemental iron, MCP joint findings, TS, or SF.

