Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2010.11929'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 175766 tokens): Title: An Image is Worth 16x16 Words: Transformers for Image Recognition at Scale
Authors: Alexey Dosovitskiy, Lucas Beyer, Alexander Kolesnikov, Dirk Weissenborn, Xiaohua Zhai, Thomas Unterthiner, Mostafa Dehghani, Matthias Minderer, Georg Heigold, Sylvain Gelly, Jakob Uszkoreit, Neil Houlsby
Link: https://huggingface.co/papers/2010.11929

Core idea and contributions:
- Vision Transformer (ViT) applies a pure Transformer directly to sequences of image patches (typically 16×16 pixels) instead of relying on convolutional neural networks (CNNs).
- When pre-trained on large datasets and fine-tuned on mid-sized or small image recognition benchmarks (e.g., ImageNet, CIFAR-100, VTAB), ViT achieves excellent performance compared to state-of-the-art CNNs.
- ViT attains these results with substantially fewer computational resources to train than comparable CNN-based models.

Method highlights:
- Images are split into fixed-size patches; each patch is linearly embedded and fed to a standard Transformer encoder.
- A class token and positional embeddings enable global reasoning across patches.
- The approach removes convolutions entirely, showing that global self-attention over patches is sufficient for strong image classification.

Empirical findings (as summarized in the provided content):
- Strong transfer learning performance across diverse benchmarks (ImageNet, CIFAR-100, VTAB).
- Competitive or superior accuracy to CNNs with better training efficiency, provided sufficient pre-training data.

Notable implementations and ecosystem (selection from the provided list):
- Google ViT checkpoints widely used for image classification and feature extraction:
  - google/vit-base-patch16-224 and google/vit-base-patch16-224-in21k
  - google/vit-base-patch16-384
  - google/vit-base-patch32-384
  - google/vit-large-patch16-224 and google/vit-large-patch16-224-in21k
  - google/vit-huge-patch14-224-in21k
- Broad framework support: Hugging Face Transformers (PyTorch/TF/JAX), and timm (e.g., timm/vit_large_patch14_clip_224.openai_ft_in12k_in1k).
- Applications beyond standard classification:
  - Falconsai/nsfw_image_detection (NSFW image classification using ViT).
  - Extensive use in multimodal systems (e.g., Google’s PaliGemma and PaliGemma 2 model families for image-to-text/image-text-to-text), which reference or build on ViT-style patch processing.
- Typical pipeline tags: image-classification and image-feature-extraction; common licensing for Google ViT checkpoints is Apache-2.0.

Takeaway:
- ViT demonstrates that Transformers operating on patch sequences can match or surpass CNNs on image recognition, especially when scaled with large pre-training data, and do so with lower training compute—establishing a new, widely adopted foundation for vision and vision-language models.
Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2103.14030'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 39696 tokens): Title: Swin Transformer: Hierarchical Vision Transformer using Shifted Windows
Authors: Ze Liu, Yutong Lin, Yue Cao, Han Hu, Yixuan Wei, Zheng Zhang, Stephen Lin, Baining Guo
Paper link: https://huggingface.co/papers/2103.14030
Code: https://github.com/microsoft/Swin-Transformer

Core idea and contributions:
- Problem: Vanilla Transformers from NLP struggle in vision due to high image resolutions and large scale variation of visual entities.
- Solution: Swin Transformer, a hierarchical Vision Transformer whose representations are computed with shifted windows.
- Shifted windowing:
  - Computes self-attention within non-overlapping local windows for efficiency.
  - Alternates window partitions by shifting them across layers to enable cross-window connections without heavy global attention.
- Hierarchical design:
  - Uses patch merging to build multi-scale feature maps, making it compatible with dense prediction tasks (detection, segmentation).
  - Achieves linear computational complexity with respect to image size.
- General-purpose backbone: Effective across classification, object detection, instance segmentation, and semantic segmentation.
- Also benefits all-MLP architectures via the same hierarchical and windowing principles.

Key results (significantly surpass state of the art at publication time):
- Image classification (ImageNet-1K): 87.3% top-1 accuracy.
- Object detection/instance segmentation (COCO test-dev): 58.7 box AP, 51.1 mask AP.
- Semantic segmentation (ADE20K val): 53.5 mIoU.
- Improvements over prior SOTA: +2.7 box AP, +2.6 mask AP on COCO; +3.2 mIoU on ADE20K.

Why it works:
- Local window attention drastically reduces cost.
- Shifted windows propagate information across windows with minimal overhead.
- Hierarchical features match the needs of dense prediction tasks and multi-scale modeling.

Notable implementations and checkpoints on Hugging Face:
- Official Microsoft Swin models (PyTorch/TF, Apache-2.0):
