Tool `Paper Search:search_arxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'Articulate Medical Intelligence Explorer AMIE conversational diagnostic AI 2024', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 5164 tokens): Summarized content:

1) Video-R2: Reinforcing Consistent and Grounded Reasoning in Multimodal Language Models (cs.CV)
- Problem: Video reasoning traces in MLLMs often look convincing but are logically inconsistent or weakly grounded in visual evidence.
- Diagnostics: Introduces Think Answer Consistency (TAC) to measure alignment between reasoning and answers, and Video Attention Score (VAS) to quantify reliance on visual vs textual cues. Across 11 video reasoning benchmarks, current models heavily rely on linguistic priors.
- Method: A dual post-training stage combining timestamp-aware supervised fine-tuning with Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), guided by a new Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR) to improve temporal precision and causal coherence.
- Results: Video-R2 yields higher TAC, VAS, and accuracy across multiple benchmarks; code, dataset, and model will be open-sourced.

2) Thinking by Doing: Building Efficient World Model Reasoning in LLMs via Multi-turn Interaction (cs.AI)
- Problem: Rigid, structured reasoning during multi-turn interaction limits efficient world-model internalization.
- Method (WMAct): Frees models from fixed reasoning structures, letting them “learn by doing.” Two key mechanisms: (1) reward rescaling based on action efficacy to reduce redundancy and promote purposeful interaction; (2) interaction frequency annealing to progressively cap allowed turns, pushing internalization of environment dynamics.
- Results: On Sokoban, Maze, and Taxi, WMAct enables single-turn solutions where previously multiple turns were needed, with strong transfer to complex environments and broader reasoning gains.

3) AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (cs.CV)
- Challenge: Audio-driven multi-person talking video generation is constrained by costly multi-person datasets and difficulty maintaining coherent interactivity.
- Method: Extensible multi-stream architecture; augments Diffusion Transformer attention with identity-aware attention that iteratively processes arbitrary numbers of identity–audio pairs. Training relies primarily on single-person videos to learn multi-person speaking patterns, with limited real multi-person clips for interactivity refinement.
- Contributions/Results: New metric and dataset to assess naturalness/interactivity; achieves strong lip sync, visual quality, and natural interactions while balancing data cost and identity scalability.

4) ThetaEvolve: Test-time Learning on Open Problems (cs.LG; cs.CL)
- Goal: Open-source, efficient alternative to AlphaEvolve that scales both in-context learning and RL at test time so models learn evolving strategies on open optimization problems.
- Features: Single LLM; large program database; batch sampling; lazy penalties to discourage stagnant outputs; optional reward shaping.
- Results: Small open-source model (DeepSeek-R1-0528-Qwen3-8B) achieves new best-known bounds on circle packing and first auto-correlation inequality. RL at test-time consistently beats inference-only baselines; RL-trained checkpoints progress faster and generalize better to unseen tasks.
- Code: https://github.com/ypwang61/ThetaEvolve

5) Wilson loops, symmetries, and selective bulk-boundary correspondence in higher-order topological insulators (cond-mat.mes-hall)
- Scope: Chiral-symmetric Bloch Hamiltonians generalizing the π-flux square lattice, including separable and nonseparable cases with extended/diagonal hopping.
- Findings: Product of subsystem chiral winding numbers predicts corner zero modes for separable systems but fails for nonseparable ones.
- New invariants: Gauge-independent mirror-filtered winding numbers for Wannier Hamiltonians; periodicized Wilson lines adapted to momentum-dependent chiral operators define invariants tied to Wannier gaps, characterizing Wannier band topology.
- Phenomena: Specific mirror-symmetry breaking causes boundary-selective gap closings; extends to 3D (hinge/corner-selective transitions).
