  - PsyRoBERTa: first psychiatry-specialized LLM trained on ~44 million psychiatric-relevant clinical notes (Denmark).
  - Outperformed three public general/medical-domain models and a logistic regression baseline for predicting psychiatric acute readmissions (AUC=0.74) and recognized psychiatric diagnoses (AUC=0.85).
  - Captured features aligning with clinical practice; highlights the value of domain-specific pretraining for leveraging unstructured psychiatric notes.

- Inferring pathogen superspreading potential using early spatial spread patterns (doi:10.1101/2025.10.28.25338965)
  - Branching-process model with US inter-county mobility; transmission heterogeneity via negative binomial with mean R0 and dispersion r (superspreading increases as r decreases).
  - Stronger superspreading slows early spatial invasion and increases variability across realizations.
  - A graph neural network can infer r from early spatial spread, robust to spatiotemporal R0 variation and underreporting.
  - Early US COVID-19: strong superspreading pre-lockdown (r=0.50, 95% CI 0.23–1.20), weaker post-lockdown (r=1.3, 0.64–3.18).
  - Offers a population-level method to quantify superspreading without detailed contact/genomic data.

- The impact of later trading hours for bars and clubs on alcohol-related ambulance call-outs and crimes in Scotland (doi:10.1101/2025.10.30.25339133)
  - Controlled interrupted time series (2015–2021) of local policy changes: Aberdeen (38 bars extended 1–3 h to 3am) and Glasgow (10 nightclubs +1 h to 4am) vs Edinburgh control.
  - Aberdeen: significant relative increases at weekend night-times in alcohol-related ambulance call-outs (+17.4%; effect size 7.061; 95% CI 3.902–10.220; p<0.001) and reported crimes (+7.9%; effect size 3.187; 95% CI 0.564–5.810; p=0.017; not robust across all sensitivity analyses).
  - Glasgow: no significant robust effects.
  - Implication: number/capacity/type of premises moderates harm—key for licensing policies.

- Sex Differences in the Protective Effect of Brain Volume: Age Attenuates Protection in Females (doi:10.1101/2025.10.30.25339193)
  - Multicenter AIS registry (n=1,103; 48% female). Primary outcome: 90-day functional independence (mRS 0–2).
  - Functional independence: 51.7% males vs 44.9% females (p=0.025).
  - PBV protective in multivariable models for males (aOR 1.30 per 100 cm³; 95% CI 1.10–1.60), not for females (aOR 1.10; 0.90–1.33).
  - Age reduced odds of independence more in females (aOR per decade 0.70; 0.62–0.79) vs males (0.85; 0.72–0.97); interaction p=0.019.
  - Age associated with larger infarcts in females (+4.22 cm³/decade; p=0.033), not males. Aging weakens brain reserve in women; include sex/PBV in outcome models.

