Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'GPT-4 assistance physician randomized controlled trial Nature Medicine 2025 10.1038/s41591-024-03456-y', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result: [{"paper_id":"39910272","title":"GPT-4 assistance for improvement of physician performance on patient care tasks: a randomized controlled trial.","authors":"Goh E; Gallo RJ; Strong E; Weng Y; Kerman H; Freed JA; Cool JA; Kanjee Z; Lane KP; Parsons AS; Ahuja N; Horvitz E; Yang D; Milstein A; Olson APJ; Hom J; Chen JH; Rodman A","abstract":"While large language models (LLMs) have shown promise in diagnostic reasoning, their impact on management reasoning, which involves balancing treatment decisions and testing strategies while managing risk, is unknown. This prospective, randomized, controlled trial assessed whether LLM assistance improves physician performance on open-ended management reasoning tasks compared to conventional resources. From November 2023 to April 2024, 92 practicing physicians were randomized to use either GPT-4 plus conventional resources or conventional resources alone to answer five expert-developed clinical vignettes in a simulated setting. All cases were based on real, de-identified patient encounters, with information revealed sequentially to mirror the nature of clinical environments. The primary outcome was the difference in total score between groups on expert-developed scoring rubrics. Secondary outcomes included domain-specific scores and time spent per case. Physicians using the LLM scored significantly higher compared to those using conventional resources (mean difference = 6.5%, 95% confidence interval (CI) = 2.7 to 10.2, P < 0.001). LLM users spent more time per case (mean difference = 119.3 s, 95% CI = 17.4 to 221.2, P = 0.02). There was no significant difference between LLM-augmented physicians and LLM alone (-0.9%, 95% CI = -9.0 to 7.2, P = 0.8). LLM assistance can improve physician management reasoning in complex clinical vignettes compared to conventional resources and should be validated in real clinical practice. ClinicalTrials.gov registration: NCT06208423 .","doi":"10.1038/s41591-024-03456-y","published_date":"2025-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39910272/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2025-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""}]
Tool `Paper Search:search_arxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'RETFound retinal foundation model generalizable disease detection 2023', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 4699 tokens): Here’s a concise synthesis of the key contributions and results across the provided papers:

- Video-R2: Reinforcing Consistent and Grounded Reasoning in MLLMs for Video (Maaz et al.; cs.CV)
  - Problem: Current video reasoning traces can be convincing yet logically inconsistent or weakly grounded in visual evidence; models over-rely on linguistic priors.
  - Diagnostics: Introduces Think Answer Consistency (TAC) to measure reasoning–answer alignment, and Video Attention Score (VAS) to quantify reliance on visual vs textual cues.
  - Method: Two-stage post-training—timestamp-aware supervised fine-tuning plus GRPO reinforcement learning with a new Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR) to encourage temporally aligned, causally coherent reasoning.
  - Results: Across 11 video reasoning benchmarks, Video-R2 improves TAC, VAS, and accuracy, indicating better temporal alignment and reasoning coherence. Code/datasets/models to be open-sourced.

- Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations (Rasheed et al.; cs.CV)
  - Paradigm: Shifts from “thinking about videos” (static context) to “thinking with videos” via an interactive Chain of Manipulations (CoM) that iteratively performs visual actions to gather/verify evidence.
  - Data/Training: Video-CoM Instruct (18K) for multi-step manipulation reasoning; SFT on 25K samples + GRPO on 3K videos.
  - RL: Reasoning-aware GRPO with step-level reasoning rewards (beyond sparse answer rewards), improving grounded, consistent reasoning.
  - Results: Strong performance on 9 video reasoning benchmarks, +3.6% average over recent SOTA with far fewer training samples; ablations show reasoning-aware rewards boost accuracy and interpretability. Code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM

- Thinking by Doing: Efficient World Model Reasoning via Multi-turn Interaction (Bao Shu et al.; cs.AI)
  - Goal: Enable LLM agents to internalize world models through efficient interaction and active reasoning (WMAct), avoiding rigid, pre-imposed reasoning structures.
  - Mechanisms:
    - Reward rescaling: Adjusts outcome reward by action efficacy to reduce redundant interactions and encourage purposeful behavior.
    - Interaction frequency annealing: Gradually reduces max interaction turns to force condensed learning and internalization of dynamics.
  - Results: On Sokoban, Maze, Taxi, WMAct solves tasks in single turns that previously needed multiple interactions; shows strong transfer to harder environments and improved performance on reasoning benchmarks.

- AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (Zhizhou Zhong et al.; cs.CV)
  - Architecture: Extends Diffusion Transformer with identity-aware attention that iteratively processes identity–audio pairs, enabling arbitrary scaling of drivable identities (multi-stream processing).
  - Training: Learns multi-person speaking patterns using solely single-person videos; refines interactivity with a small set of real multi-person clips.
  - Evaluation: Introduces a targeted metric and dataset to assess naturalness and interactivity.
  - Results: Achieves strong lip-sync, visual quality, and natural interactivity; balances data cost with identity scalability.

- ThetaEvolve: Test-time Learning on Open Problems (Yiping Wang et al.; cs.LG/cs.CL)
  - Contribution: Open-source framework extending AlphaEvolve to support both in-context learning and RL at test time, enabling continual learning on open optimization problems with a single LLM.
  - Features: Large program database for exploration, batch sampling for throughput, lazy penalties to discourage stagnation, optional reward shaping; efficient and simplified pipeline.
  - Results: First to let a small open-source model (DeepSeek-R1-0528-Qwen3-8B) achieve new best-known bounds on circle packing and the first auto-correlation inequality. Across two models/four tasks, RL at test-time consistently outperforms inference-only, and RL checkpoints generalize better 
Tool `Paper Search:search_arxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'Articulate Medical Intelligence Explorer AMIE clinical conversations 2024 Nature', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 5286 tokens): - Thinking by Doing: Building Efficient World Model Reasoning in LLMs via Multi-turn Interaction (WMAct)
  - Authors: Bao Shu et al.
  - Problem: Rigid, structured reasoning in multi-turn LLM agents limits active learning and efficient world-model internalization.
  - Approach: WMAct enables “thinking by doing” with two mechanisms: (1) reward rescaling based on action efficacy to reduce redundancy and incentivize purposeful interaction; (2) interaction frequency annealing that gradually lowers allowed interaction turns to force internalization of dynamics instead of over-reliance on environment cues.
  - Results: On Sokoban, Maze, and Taxi, WMAct learns effective world-model reasoning that solves tasks in a single turn that previously needed multiple, and transfers well to more complex environments, improving performance on multiple reasoning benchmarks.

- AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement
  - Authors: Zhizhou Zhong et al.
  - Problem: Audio-driven multi-person talking video generation suffers from scarce multi-person data and difficulty ensuring coherent interactivity across identities.
  - Approach: Extensible multi-stream Diffusion Transformer with identity-aware attention iteratively processes identity–audio pairs for arbitrary identity scaling. Training relies primarily on single-person videos to learn multi-person speaking patterns and uses few real multi-person clips to refine interactivity. Introduces a targeted metric and dataset for naturalness and interactivity evaluation.
  - Results: Strong lip sync, visual quality, and natural interactivity with favorable trade-off between data cost and scalability.

- ThetaEvolve: Test-time Learning on Open Problems
  - Authors: Yiping Wang et al.
  - Problem: Prior evolving systems (e.g., AlphaEvolve) are closed-source, ensemble-heavy, inference-only; models don’t internalize evolving strategies.
  - Approach: Open-source framework that scales in-context learning and reinforcement learning (RL) at test time using a single LLM, a large program database, batch sampling for throughput, lazy penalties to avoid stagnation, and optional reward shaping. Enables continual learning during problem improvement.
  - Results: A small open-source model (DeepSeek-R1-0528-Qwen3-8B) achieves new best-known bounds on circle packing and the first auto-correlation inequality (from AlphaEvolve’s set). Across two models and four open tasks, test-time RL consistently beats inference-only baselines; RL-trained checkpoints progress faster and generalize to unseen tasks. Code: https://github.com/ypwang61/ThetaEvolve

- Visual Generation Tuning (VGT)
  - Authors: Jiahao Guo et al.
  - Problem: VLMs trained for multimodal understanding may harbor untapped generative potential; need efficient tuning for autoregressive visual generation without heavy pixel-VAEs.
  - Approach: VGT performs efficient visual-generation tuning of pretrained VLMs, aligning semantic encoders with pixel-decoders via VGT-AE, avoiding entangled pixel-level VAEs. Achieves ~20× faster convergence for continuous-space autoregressive modeling.
  - Results: Image reconstruction 26.67 PSNR and 0.50 rFID at 28× compression, outperforming specialized VAEs. Visual generation sets state-of-the-art among autoregressive models: 0.77 on GenEval and 78.73 on DPG-Bench. Demonstrates scalability and applicability to diverse VLMs. Code: https://github.com/hustvl/VGT

- Kinetic Mixing and the Phantom Illusion: Axion-Dilaton Quintessence in Light of DESI DR2
  - Authors: Michael W. Toomey et al.
  - Problem: DESI BAO hints at dynamical dark energy with past phantom behavior; can a string-motivated kinetically mixed axion–dilaton (KMIX) model explain this without true phantom?
  - Approach: Build a normalizing-flow pipeline that (i) learns a theory-informed prior on (w0, wa) from KMIX realizations and (ii) maps CPL parameters back to KMIX parameters. Importance-sample precomputed CPL chains to obtai
