Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'ambient scribe generative AI randomized 2024 2025', 'max_results': 15} on Paper Search succeeded. Result: [{"paper_id":"40675605","title":"The Influence of Artificial Intelligence Scribes on Clinician Experience and Efficiency among Pediatric Subspecialists: A Rapid, Randomized Quality Improvement Trial.","authors":"Shin HS; Williams H; Braykov N; Jahan A; Meller J; Orenstein EW","abstract":"Artificial intelligence (AI) scribes may reduce the documentation burden and improve clinician experience through generative AI automatically producing provider note sections from recordings of patient-provider encounters.We aimed to examine the impact of AI scribes on clinician experience, clinician efficiency, and business efficiency measures among pediatric subspecialty physicians.We randomized pediatric subspecialty providers with ≥0.5 clinical full-time equivalent and stable electronic health record (EHR) log metrics to use Microsoft/Nuance Digital Ambient eXperience (DAX) Copilot from May 1, 2024, to July 31, 2024 (intervention group) or controls. Using difference-in-differences, we compared quantitative measures of subjective clinician experience using the KLAS Net EHR Experience survey, objective measures of clinician efficiency from EHR logs (e.g., pajama time), and business efficiency measures. At-the-elbow support checked in with intervention providers approximately weekly, and we assessed the sentiment of qualitative comments.Twelve providers were randomized to the intervention and 11 to the control group. One intervention provider stopped using DAX due to ineffectiveness. In the intervention group, DAX was used to populate one or more characters in 53% of visit notes (range across providers: 10.6-98.2%). Nine intervention and eight control providers completed pre- and postsurveys. KLAS Net EHR Experience improved among intervention providers from 52.6 (70th percentile) to 75.2 (99th percentile) but dropped from 37.3 (38th percentile) to 30 (14th percentile) among control providers. Experiencing burnout dropped from 8 (89%) to 5 (56%) among intervention providers but remained stable at 3 (38%) in the control group. There was no significant change to pajama time (-9.4 minutes per scheduled day, 95% CI: -41.2 to +22.4), time in notes per encounter (+0.2 minutes per note, 95% CI: -6.6 to +6.9), or work Relative Value Units (wRVUs) per encounter (-0.03, 95% CI: -0.5 to +0.44). Of 48 qualitative comments, 69% had a positive sentiment, 15% neutral, and 17% negative.Among pediatric subspecialists, AI scribes improved clinician experience and burnout without changing charting time or EHR work outside work hours.","doi":"10.1055/a-2657-8087","published_date":"2025-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40675605/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2025-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""},{"paper_id":"40672471","title":"A Randomized-Clinical Trial of Two Ambient Artificial Intelligence Scribes: Measuring Documentation Efficiency and Physician Burnout.","authors":"Lukac PJ; Turner W; Vangala S; Chin AT; Khalili J; Shih YT; Sarkisian C; Cheng EM; Mafi JN","abstract":"Ambient artificial intelligence (AI) scribes record patient encounters and generate visit notes almost instantaneously, representing a promising solution to documentation burden and associated physician burnout. Despite swift and widespread adoption of AI scribes, their impacts have not been examined in randomized-clinical trials.","doi":"10.1101/2025.07.10.25331333","published_date":"2025-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40672471/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2025-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""}]
Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'radiology large language model prospective evaluation 2024 2025', 'max_results': 15} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 3131 tokens): Overview: Ten recent studies explore large language models (LLMs) and related AI in radiology and clinical workflows, focusing on information extraction, report interpretation, workflow optimization, clinical decision support, patient communication, education, and monitoring of deployed AI.

- Large language models for AAA data extraction (Flanagan et al.)
  - Objective: Evaluate a generalized LLM to extract abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA)–related data from abdominal imaging reports in a large real-world database without task-specific training.
  - Rationale: AAA surveillance is costly and labor-intensive; prior NLP requires bespoke training.
  - Takeaway: Title indicates accurate extraction; abstract emphasizes eliminating task-based training to reduce manual review burden.

- Automated BI-RADS from narrative breast MRI reports (Tekcan Sanli et al.)
  - Objective: Assess GPT-4o’s performance in assigning BI-RADS categories and recommending management from free-text MRI reports lacking explicit BI-RADS.
  - Focus: Interpretation of unstructured breast MRI narratives for standardized categorization and clinical recommendations.

- GBC pre-operative T-stage discrimination with ML and DeepSeek-R1 (Chae et al.)
  - Objectives: (i) Test whether routine blood biomarkers can distinguish early T stages via ML; (ii) compare DeepSeek-R1’s discrimination using radiology-report text alone vs text plus biomarker values.
  - Domain: Multimodal inputs (text + labs) for staging gallbladder cancer prior to surgery.

- LLMs for postdeployment monitoring of FDA-approved AI (PE detection use case) (Sorin et al.)
  - Problem: AI performance can diverge post-deployment; PDM is challenging for ongoing QA.
  - Objective: Explore LLM-enabled approaches to monitor and assess FDA-cleared pulmonary embolism detection AI in production settings.

- AI to enhance radiologist productivity in MRI: narrative review (Nair et al.)
  - Methods: Searched multiple databases and grey literature for English-language studies (2000–Nov 15, 2024); 67 studies included.
  - Themes: (1) Reducing scan times, (2) Automating segmentation, (3) Optimizing workflow and triage, (4) Decreasing reading times, (5) General time savings/workload reduction.
  - Techniques: Predominantly CNNs (e.g., ResNet, U-Net); some ML automation; emerging use of LLMs.
  - Findings: Most report efficiency and accuracy gains, but limited external validation and heterogeneous datasets impede broad adoption.
  - Conclusion: AI can streamline MRI workflows; calls for prospective validation and standardized metrics for safe, equitable deployment.

- Can an LLM write acceptable dental board-style questions? (Kim & Kim)
  - Objective: Compare LLM-generated national dental board–style questions vs human-authored items using item analysis.
  - Design: Cross-sectional prospective study.
  - Focus: Educational content generation quality and psychometrics.

- RAG-powered chatbot for pre-CT informed consent (Busch et al.)
  - Design: Prospective comparative randomized study; 86 patients scheduled for CT (Nov–Dec 2024) randomized to PIA chatbot (n=43) vs standard physician consultation (n=43).
  - Outcomes:
    - Information clarity: PIA 8.64±1.69 vs control 8.86±1.28 (p=0.82).
    - Overall comprehension: PIA 8.81±1.40 vs control 8.93±1.61 (p=0.35).
    - Alleviating concerns: Physicians superior (8.30±2.63 vs 6.46±3.29; p=0.003).
    - Subsequent consultation time reduced with PIA: median 120 s (IQR 100–140) vs 195 s (IQR 170–220); p=0.04.
    - Expert review: Two radiologists rated PIA chats highly for overall quality, evidence, usefulness/relevance, consistency, and up-to-dateness.
  - Conclusion: RAG-based PIA effectively delivers pre-CT information and reduces physician time, though physicians better address patient worries.

