- Title: Outcomes of Treatment-Resistant Neovascular Age-Related Macular Degeneration Switched from Aflibercept to Faricimab
- Authors: Raimondi R; Felfel T; Bogdanova-Bennet A; Varma D; Habib M; Kotagiri A; Steel DH; Grinton M
- Year/DOI: 2024; 10.1016/j.oret.2023.11.015
- Background: Faricimab effective in treatment-naive nAMD; outcomes after switching from aflibercept in suboptimal responders are less clear.
- Aim/Design: Assess anatomical and functional outcomes after switching to faricimab in aflibercept-treated, suboptimally responsive nAMD patients
Tool `Paper Search:search_medrxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'randomized controlled trial large language model clinician 2024 2025', 'max_results': 15} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 10912 tokens): Here are concise summaries of each paper, preserving key methods, samples, metrics, and findings:

- Evaluating large language models for predicting psychiatric acute readmissions from clinical notes of population-based EHR (PsyRoBERTa)
  - Built PsyRoBERTa, the first LLM specialized for clinical psychiatry, pretrained on ~44 million psychiatric-relevant clinical notes from the eastern half of Denmark (population-based, non-English).
  - Compared against three publicly available models (general/medical-domain) and a logistic regression baseline for predicting acute psychiatric readmissions; also evaluated diagnosis recognition.
  - Results: Outperformed prior models for readmission prediction (AUC=0.74); recognized psychiatric diagnoses well (AUC=0.85).
  - Takeaway: Domain-specific pretraining is critical; LLMs can leverage clinical notes to enhance psychiatric outcome prediction and capture features aligned with clinical practice.

- Inferring pathogen superspreading potential using early spatial spread patterns
  - Modeled early spread in US counties via a branching process with inter-county mobility; secondary cases followed a negative binomial with mean R0 and dispersion r (superspreading measure).
  - Simulations: Stronger superspreading (lower r) slows early spatial invasion and increases variability across realizations.
  - Used a graph neural network to infer r from early spatial patterns; robust to spatiotemporal R0 variation and underreporting.
  - Applied to early US COVID-19: strong superspreading pre-lockdown (r=0.50; 95% CI: 0.23–1.20), weaker after (r=1.3; 0.64–3.18).
  - Contribution: Enables quantification of superspreading from population-level spread data.

- The impact of later trading hours for bars and clubs on alcohol-related ambulance call-outs and crimes in Scotland: a controlled interrupted time series study
  - Natural experiment (2015–2021), controlled interrupted time series vs. Edinburgh.
  - Policy: Aberdeen—38 bars extended 1–3 hours up to 3am; Glasgow—10 nightclubs extended 1 hour to 4am.
  - Primary outcome: weekend night-time alcohol-related ambulance call-outs; secondary: weekend night-time crimes.
  - Aberdeen: significant relative increases—ambulance call-outs +17.4% (effect size=7.061; 95% CI: 3.902–10.220; p<0.001); crimes +7.9% (effect=3.187; 95% CI: 0.564–5.810; p=0.017; not robust in sensitivity).
  - Glasgow: no significant, robust effects.
  - Implication: Harms rise with later hours where more/larger premises extend; local context moderates effects; informs licensing policy.

- Sex Differences in the Protective Effect of Brain Volume: Age Attenuates Protection in Females
  - Multicenter registry of AIS patients (n=1,103; 48% female; median age 66); automated PBV from non-contrast CT; DWI-FIV from 24–48h MRI.
  - Outcome: 90-day functional independence (mRS 0–2): 51.7% males vs 44.9% females (p=0.025).
  - Univariable: higher PBV associated with independence in both sexes (males OR 1.30; 95% CI: 1.11–1.53; females OR 1.35; 1.13–1.61).
  - Multivariable (VIF mean=1.32): PBV protective in males (aOR 1.30 per 100 cm3; 1.10–1.60), not in females (aOR 1.10; 0.90–1.33).
  - Age effect: per decade, odds reduced 30% in females (aOR 0.70; 0.62–0.79) vs 15% in males (aOR 0.85; 0.72–0.97; interaction p=0.019); age associated with larger infarcts in females (+4.22 cm3/decade; p=0.033).
  - Conclusion: Aging diminishes brain reserve benefit in women; include sex and brain volume in models.

