Tool `Paper Search:search_biorxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'vision transformer medical imaging benchmark 2024 2025', 'max_results': 15} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 8531 tokens): - Programmed Delayed Splicing: Using hybrid capture of chromatin-associated pre-mRNAs and deep sequencing of the NF-κB transcriptome after transcriptional “freeze,” many introns splice tens–hundreds of times slower than average. Conserved weak splice donor sites act as timing elements; strengthening them (even by 2 nt) boosts expression in minigene assays. Deep learning feature attribution identifies additional intronic/exonic cis-elements and non-canonical motifs contributing to slow splicing, supporting timed pre-mRNA maturation as a layer controlling inflammatory gene onset and amplitude, potentially generalizable to other rapid-response programs.

- Ddx3x in B-cell development: Conditional deletion of Ddx3x in developing mouse B cells (Mb1-Cre females) eliminates mature peripheral B cells via a block at the pro-B stage. Hemizygous deletion in males (Vav1- or Mb1-Cre) yields milder reductions and marginal zone skewing, suggesting partial Ddx3y or male compensation. Mechanistically, Ddx3x loss reduces Brwd1 in pre-B cells, fails to curtail proliferation, and impairs Igκ rearrangement, shifting peripheral light-chain usage toward λ. Ddx3x is essential for cell-cycle and epigenetic programs enabling immunoglobulin rearrangement.

- ErbB3 and endocytic sorting: In breast epithelial cells, ErbB3 sorts internalized integrin β1 and transferrin receptor for recycling (likely Rab4-dependent) independent of ligand signaling. ErbB3 loss blocks recycling, redirects integrin β1 to lysosomal degradation and extracellular vesicle (EV) secretion, reduces surface integrin β1, and impairs collective migration. EVs from ErbB3-depleted cells enhance wild-type cell motility. ErbB3 promotes assembly of the Arf6–GGA3–Rabaptin5 complex to facilitate early recycling and suppress EV release, highlighting active trafficking roles for pseudo-RTKs.

- Longevity records and data quality: In the U.S., supercentenarian records are predicted by absent vital registration; state adoption of birth certificates coincides with a 69–82% drop in records. In Italy, England, and France, remarkable age prevalence correlates with poverty, low income, high crime, worse health, shorter life expectancy, fewer 90+, and remote/overseas regions. Only 18% of validated supercentenarians have birth certificates (0% in the U.S.); birthdates cluster on days divisible by five. Noted “blue zones” align with poorer, lower-literacy areas. Patterns indicate widespread fraud/error as primary drivers of extreme age records.

- Multisensory sequence prediction dynamics: MEG during audiovisual serial prediction reveals theta-band (5–7 Hz) coupling across cingulate, premotor, prefrontal, and superior temporal regions; behavior varies with inter-regional phase delays. Two beta-band clusters (11–16 and 16–22 Hz) show stronger power when adjacent sequence items match the current one; they spatially overlap the theta network and interact via cross-frequency coupling. Results support multi-timescale oscillatory coupling as key for multisensory predictions.

- Nuclear GAPDH and cardiac hypertrophy: In mouse pressure overload (TAC) and human dilated hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, GAPDH translocates to nuclei. Cys150-dependent nuclear translocation enhances BRG1–HDAC2 interaction, shifting Myh7/Myh6 toward a fetal program and driving hypertrophy. A selective inhibitor (RR compound) and genetic interventions block nuclear translocation and ameliorate hypertrophy/dysfunction in vivo. Reveals a stress-induced “moonlighting” GAPDH cascade linking chromatin remodeling to pathological growth.

