Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'retinal foundation model generalizable disease detection Nature 2023', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 2864 tokens): Here are the key points from each paper, preserving the most important information in under 1000 tokens:

1) Non-invasive biopsy diagnosis of diabetic kidney disease via deep learning applied to retinal images: a population-based study (Meng et al., 2025; DOI: 10.1016/j.landig.2025.02.008; PubMed 40312169)
- Aim: Develop and validate an AI deep learning system using retinal fundus images to detect diabetic kidney disease (DKD) and distinguish isolated diabetic nephropathy from non-diabetic kidney disease (NDKD).
- Rationale: DKD screening accessibility and differentiating DN vs NDKD are major challenges; a retinal image-based, non-invasive approach could serve as a surrogate for biopsy triage.
- Design: Population-based, multicohort development/validation (details not provided in abstract).
- Significance: Proposes a scalable, non-invasive tool for DKD detection and differentiation, potentially improving screening and clinical decision-making.

2) Comparison of retinal degeneration treatment with different mesenchymal stem cells, human induced pluripotent stem cells and RPE cells in a rat retinal degeneration model (Liu et al., 2023; DOI: 10.1186/s12967-023-04785-1; PubMed 38098048)
- Aim: Compare subretinal transplantation efficacy among hADSCs, hAFSCs, hBMSCs, hDPSCs, hiPSCs, and hiPSC-derived RPE in rat retinal degeneration (RD).
- Outcomes: Protection, paracrine effects, and treatment efficiency.
- Context: Multiple stem cell types are in RD trials; optimal cell type remains unclear.
- Significance: Head-to-head preclinical evaluation to guide cell selection for RD therapy.

3) Fe-curcumin nanozyme-mediated immunosuppression and anti-inflammation in experimental autoimmune uveitis (Jiang et al., 2023; DOI: 10.1186/s40824-023-00451-1; PubMed 38087367)
- Aim: Develop and test a Fe–curcumin nanozyme for immunosuppression/anti-inflammation in EAU, an autoimmune/auto-inflammatory, ROS-aggravated uveitis.
- Rationale: Conventional hormone therapy has substantial adverse effects; need safer, effective alternatives.
- Mechanism: Nanozyme likely scavenges ROS and modulates immune pathways (details not specified here).
- Significance: Suggests a nanozyme-based therapy with potential efficacy and reduced side effects.

4) Clinical evaluation of deep learning systems for assisting in the diagnosis of the epiretinal membrane grade in general ophthalmologists (Yan et al., 2024; DOI: 10.1038/s41433-023-02765-9; PubMed 37848677)
- Aim: Develop AI systems to grade epiretinal membrane (ERM) on OCT and clinically evaluate benefits and risks in general ophthalmology practice.
- Background: ERM is common in >60 years (prevalence 34.1%); grading via OCT is standard.
- Design: Comparative clinical experiment assessing AI assistance.
- Significance: Assesses real-world AI support for ERM grading, focusing on diagnostic performance and safety.

5) Optic disc detection based on fully convolutional network and weighted matrix recovery model (Wang et al., 2023; DOI: 10.1007/s11517-023-02891-2; PubMed 37668892)
- Aim: Weakly supervised optic disc segmentation in complex fundus images.
- Method: Combines FCN-derived top-down semantic priors with bottom-up background priors in a weighted low-rank matrix recovery (WLRR) model. Uses SLIC superpixels to build a feature matrix; decomposes into sparse (disc) and low-rank (background) components.
- Data: DRISHTI-GS and IDRiD datasets.
- Results: More accurate optic disc segmentation than existing weakly supervised methods.
- Significance: Improves robustness to vessel interference and complex backgrounds.

6) CLRD: Collaborative Learning for Retinopathy Detection Using Fundus Images (Gao et al., 2023; DOI: 10.3390/bioengineering10080978; PubMed 37627863)
- Aim: Enhance retinopathy detection under limited/imbalanced fundus data.
