 FINAL SOLUTION:
2025-12-01 10:33:26,844 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Here’s a concise “what’s actually moving the needle” synthesis you can drop into a presentation, with fresh, citable evidence. I grouped the findings into 6 concrete trends and included links to primary sources.

Executive takeaways (what to highlight)
- Grounded, interactive reasoning is the new frontier. Models that “think with” the input (videos, environments) and learn at test time are measurably more accurate and trustworthy than passive, text-only reasoning.
- Agentic learning reduces reliance on long, rigid chains-of-thought. Carefully designed interaction schedules and rewards push models to internalize world dynamics and solve tasks in fewer steps.
- Capability-per-dollar is improving fast. Benchmarks should report cost alongside accuracy; algorithmic efficiency gains are large and continuing.
- Multimodal generation is scaling to realistic, multi-actor scenarios with less data. New training strategies and attention mechanisms enable coherent multi-speaker interactivity using mostly single-speaker data.
- Evaluation is maturing beyond accuracy. New diagnostics quantify whether a model’s reasoning is logically consistent and actually grounded in the input, not just plausible-sounding text.
- Clinical adoption is expanding (documentation, info extraction, empathy), but external validation, calibration, and bias remain the blockers; guidelines are emerging for safe use.

What’s new, with evidence you can cite
1) Interactive multimodal reasoning (models “think with videos,” not just about them)
- Video-R2 identifies and fixes two failure modes in video MLLMs—logic–answer inconsistency and weak visual grounding—using new diagnostics:
  - Think–Answer Consistency (TAC): does the reasoning support the answer?
  - Video Attention Score (VAS): is the model relying on visual evidence vs. language priors?
  - Method: timestamp-aware supervised fine-tuning + GRPO reinforcement learning with a Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR).
  - Evidence: Across 11 benchmarks, Video-R2 raises TAC, VAS, and accuracy, yielding more temporally grounded, causally coherent answers.
  - Cite: Maaz et al., Video-R2, arXiv:2511.23478 (code/data/model to be released)

- Video-CoM shifts from “once-encode, reason in text” to interactive visual reasoning via a Chain of Manipulations (rewatch, refocus, verify).
  - Trained with small data (≈25K SFT + 3K RL videos) and step-level, reasoning-aware rewards (not just answer-only rewards).
  - Evidence: +3.6% average over recent SOTA on 9 video reasoning benchmarks with strong interpretability gains.
  - Cite: Rasheed et al., Video-CoM, arXiv:2511.23477; code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM

2) Agentic LLMs that internalize world models and learn efficiently
- WMAct (Thinking by Doing) shows that carefully shaping interaction and rewards yields efficient world-model reasoning without rigid, verbose chains.
  - Mechanisms: reward rescaling by action efficacy (discourages redundant steps); interaction-frequency annealing (gradually cap turns to force internalization).
  - Evidence: On Sokoban, Maze, Taxi, tasks solvable in single turns that previously needed multiple; strong transfer to harder settings and broader reasoning suites.
  - Cite: Shu et al., Thinking by Doing (WMAct), arXiv:2511.23476

- Test-time learning on open problems (ThetaEvolve) demonstrates continuous strategy improvement using a single LLM with in-context learning + RL at test time.
  - Evidence: Small open models achieve new best-known bounds (e.g., circle packing; first auto-correlation inequality); RL at test time consistently beats inference-only; learned strategies transfer to unseen tasks.
  - Cite: Wang et al., ThetaEvolve, arXiv:2511.23473; code: https://github.com/ypwang61/ThetaEvolve

3) Capability-per-dollar and algorithmic efficiency
- The Price of Progress compiles the largest dataset of benchmark run prices and finds:
  - For frontier models, the price to hit a given benchmark score fell ~5–10× per year across knowledge, reasoning, math, and coding.
  - After controlling for hardware and open-model competition, algorithmic efficiency improved ~3×/year.
  - Recommendation: report cost alongside scores; capability-per-dollar matters for real-world impact.
  - Cite: Gundlach et al., The Price of Progress (algorithmic efficiency), arXiv preprint (2025)

4) Scaling coherent multimodal generation with less data
- AnyTalker scales multi-person, audio-driven talking video generation with coherent interactivity across identities—using mostly single-person training data.
  - Architecture: extensible multi-stream Diffusion Transformer with identity-aware attention that iteratively processes identity–audio pairs.
  - Contributions: introduces a dataset/metric for naturalness and interactivity.
  - Evidence: Strong lip sync, visual quality, and natural interactivity with favorable data–scalability trade-offs.
  - Cite: Zhong et al., AnyTalker, arXiv:2511.23475

- Visual Generation Tuning (VGT) unlocks generation in pretrained VLMs efficiently.
  - Approach: align semantic encoders with pixel decoders (continuous AR modeling), achieving ≈20× faster convergence and strong reconstruction/generation metrics.
  - Cite: Guo et al., Visual Generation Tuning, arXiv:2511.23469; code: https://github.com/hustvl/VGT

5) Better benchmarks and diagnostics for reasoning quality
- Beyond accuracy, TAC and VAS (Video-R2) and step-level reasoning rewards (Video-CoM) are concrete tools to measure/shape:
  - internal consistency (reasoning supports answer),
  - grounding (attention to relevant evidence),
  - temporal coherence (timestamp alignment), and
  - interpretable, causal chains of evidence collection.

6) Healthcare: promising early deployments, but validation gaps remain
- Real-world use is growing in:
  - Ambient clinical documentation (reducing after-hours EHR “WOW” burden),
  - Information extraction from clinical text (e.g., imaging reports),
  - Patient communication and empathy (some studies report AI chatbots can match or exceed human empathy in specific settings).
- Governance and safety: oncology guidance (ESMO/ELCAP) and reviews highlight the need for:
  - external validation, calibration reporting, bias assessment,
  - transparent decision curves, and rigorous deployment reporting.
- Cite: multiple 2024–2025 reviews and guidance (PubMed-indexed): LLMs in clinical workflows (Frontiers in Digital Health, 2025), ESMO guidance on LLMs in oncology (ELCAP), scoping reviews on primary care and sentiment analysis, and systematic reviews in specific clinical domains (e.g., GDM prediction) emphasizing external validation and bias.

How to frame your project (suggested slide structure)
- Slide 1: Three big shifts
  - From passive to interactive reasoning (Video-CoM, Video-R2)
  - From rigid prompts to agentic learning (WMAct, ThetaEvolve)
  - From peak accuracy to capability-per-dollar (Price of Progress)
- Slide 2: Evidence snapshots
  - 3 bullets with concrete numbers: +3.6% over SOTA with 25K+3K videos; TAC/VAS gains across 11 benchmarks; ~3×/yr algorithmic efficiency after controls.
- Slide 3: Why it matters for real deployments
  - Fewer steps and grounded reasoning → reliability, lower cost, better UX
  - Healthcare: feasible use cases today + what’s missing (external validation, calibration, bias)
- Slide 4: What to watch next
  - Open-sourced code/datasets (Video-CoM, ThetaEvolve) accelerating reproducibility
  - Diagnostics becoming part of benchmarks (TAC/VAS, step-level rewards)
  - Interactive agents across modalities (vision, tools, environments)

