Tool `Paper Search:search_arxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'mixture-of-experts survey 2024', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 5410 tokens): - From metallicity distributions to mutual information: A new perspective on stellar halo assembly (Amit Mondal; Biswajit Pandey)
  - Uses five Milky Way–mass halos from Aquarius simulations to quantify angular–metallicity coupling via mutual information (MI) between angular position and metallicity.
  - Splits stars by median metallicity; metallicity distribution functions (MDFs) span single-peaked to broad/bimodal, reflecting stochastic assembly and contributions from few massive vs many small progenitors.
  - Low-metallicity stars (from disrupted satellites) show higher spatial anisotropy and stronger angular clustering than metal-rich stars.
  - Removing bound satellites reduces anisotropy substantially; high-metallicity stars remain marginally more anisotropic, consistent with centrally deposited massive progenitor debris.
  - MI increases with radius and saturates in outskirts; deviations from randomized controls are concentrated in the inner halo, indicating residual spatial–chemical coupling from incomplete phase mixing.
  - Argues information-theoretic diagnostics are powerful for quantifying chemical complexity and comparing simulations with upcoming high-D Galactic data.

- ML Researchers Support Openness in Peer Review But Are Concerned About Resubmission Bias (Vishisht Rao et al.)
  - Surveyed 2,385 ML reviewers/authors/participants on open review policies.
  - Preferences: >80% support releasing reviews for accepted papers and public comments; only 27.1% support releasing rejected manuscripts.
  - Perceived benefits: improved public understanding (75.3%), reviewer education (57.8%), fairness (56.6%), incentives for high-quality reviews (48.0%).
  - Key concerns: resubmission bias (ranked top impact by 41%, mentioned in >50% of free responses), fear of reviewer de-anonymization (33.2%), potential commenting abuse.
  - AI: respondents think openness deters “AI slop” submissions (71.9%) and AI-generated reviews (38.9%); venues generating official AI reviews split opinion (56.0% opposed, 44.0% supportive).
  - AI-assisted analysis of 4,244 reviews: ICLR (fully open) has higher correctness and completeness than NeurIPS (partially open); effect sizes small/very small; no significant difference in substantiation. Dataset released.

- Hunyuan-GameCraft-2: Instruction-following Interactive Game World Model (Junshu Tang et al.)
  - Introduces instruction-driven interaction for generative game world modeling; control via natural language, keyboard, or mouse.
  - Defines “interactive video data” and automates conversion of large unstructured text–video pairs into causally aligned interactive datasets.
  - Built on a 14B image-to-video MoE foundation with a text-driven interaction injection mechanism for precise control over camera, character, and environment dynamics.
  - Proposes InterBench to benchmark interaction quality.
  - Generates temporally coherent, causally grounded interactive game videos responsive to free-form instructions (e.g., “open the door,” “draw a torch,” “trigger an explosion”).

- La ley del descenso tendencial de la tasa de ganancia: Evidencia empírica para la economía española (Iván López-Espejo)
  - 1960–2024 Spanish economy study converting National Accounts into Marxist variables: constant capital (c), variable capital (v), surplus value (pv).
  - Constructs constant-price series for organic composition (q), rate of surplus value (pv′), and profit rate (g′).
  - Finds sustained increase in q and slight decrease in pv′, leading to a tendential decline in g′ with cyclical fluctuations linked to crises.
  - Empirically supports Marx’s law of the tendency of the rate of profit to fall in Spain and highlights structural profitability dynamics.

- Consensus based Algorithm for Nonparametric Detection of Star Clusters (CANDiSC) (C O Obasi et al.)
  - Automated, homogeneous cluster detection for VVV/VVVX near-IR surveys using a consensus of kernel density estimation, DBSCAN, and nearest-neighbour density.
