- From metallicity distributions to mutual information: A new perspective on stellar halo assembly
  - Data/Method: Five Milky Way–mass Aquarius halos; information-theoretic analysis using mutual information (MI) between angular position and metallicity; stars split at median metallicity; study MDFs, anisotropy, and angular–metallicity coupling vs radius.
  - Findings: Diverse MDFs reveal stochastic assembly (from dominant massive progenitors to multimodal accretion histories). Low-metallicity stars show higher anisotropy/clustering; removing bound satellites reduces anisotropy, but high-metallicity stars remain marginally more anisotropic (massive progenitor debris). MI rises with radius then saturates; MI excess over randomized controls mostly in inner halo, indicating incomplete phase mixing.
  - Implication: Info-theoretic diagnostics effectively quantify chemical complexity and aid sim–survey comparisons.

