2025-12-01 10:40:17,849 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Here’s a concise, evidence-based roll‑up of your dad’s cardiovascular profile using the data you provided and standardized, validated calculators.

What we computed from his numbers
- BMI and body size:
  - BMI: 26.1 kg/m² (Overweight). BSA: 1.97 m². Source: WHO BMI categories.
- Kidney function:
  - eGFR (CKD‑EPI): 69.2 mL/min/1.73 m². This is mildly reduced for age but does not by itself define CKD without albuminuria or other kidney damage markers. KDIGO defines CKD as eGFR <60 for ≥3 months and/or evidence of kidney damage. 
- Creatinine clearance (for med dosing context):
  - Cockcroft–Gault CrCl: 68.8 mL/min (using adjusted body weight due to BMI >25).
- Lipids (as provided; note non‑HDL is atherogenic cholesterol measure):
  - Total cholesterol 220 mg/dL, HDL 50 mg/dL → non‑HDL = 170 mg/dL.
- Blood pressure:
  - Systolic 140 mmHg, on treatment (hypertension present).
- Serum sodium (corrected for glucose 120 mg/dL):
  - Corrected Na+: 135.3–135.5 mEq/L (normal 135–145), using Katz and Hillier correction factors.
- Glucose 120 mg/dL:
  - If fasting, this is in the prediabetes range (100–125 mg/dL). If non‑fasting, it’s nondiagnostic; confirm with fasting plasma glucose or HbA1c.

10‑year cardiovascular risk estimates
- AHA “PREVENT” equations (integrated ASCVD risk; includes kidney function and treated BP):
  - 10‑year ASCVD risk: 10.2% → Intermediate risk category (7.5–19.9%).
  - Inputs: age 60, male, TC 220 mg/dL (5.69 mmol/L), HDL 50 mg/dL (1.29 mmol/L), SBP 140 on therapy, nonsmoker, no diabetes, eGFR ≈69.
- Framingham risk (general CVD):
  - 10‑year risk ≈ 13.8%. This older tool often gives a somewhat higher estimate and can over/under‑predict by population; use mainly for context.

Atrial fibrillation (AF) considerations
- Stroke risk if AF were present (CHA2DS2‑VASc):
  - Score: 1 (male; hypertension only) → estimated ischemic stroke risk ≈ 0.6% per year. Anticoagulation decisions depend on confirmed AF; with score 1 in men, anticoagulation is generally considered optional/individualized.
- AF risk itself: We can’t produce a validated personal AF risk number without additional variables used by AF‑specific prediction models (e.g., CHARGE‑AF requires race, exact height/weight, SBP, BP meds, smoking, diabetes, HF). That said, age, hypertension, and being overweight are established AF risk factors. If there are AF symptoms (palpitations, irregular pulse), evaluation is warranted.

What these results mean and what to discuss with his clinician
- Lipid management: Intermediate 10‑year ASCVD risk (≈10%) generally supports starting at least a moderate‑intensity statin after clinician–patient discussion, provided LDL‑C is 70–189 mg/dL (obtain a full lipid panel with LDL‑C and triglycerides). Target ≥30% LDL‑C reduction (≥50% if higher‑intensity chosen or if risk enhancers are present). Coronary artery calcium (CAC) scoring can help refine the decision if there’s uncertainty.
- Blood pressure: SBP 140 mmHg on therapy meets ACC/AHA Stage 2 hypertension. Guideline BP goal is <130/80 mmHg for most adults with hypertension. Home BP monitoring helps confirm control and guide adjustments.
- Glucose: Confirm whether 120 mg/dL was fasting. If fasting, this is prediabetes; order HbA1c (5.7–6.4% prediabetes; ≥6.5% diabetes) or repeat fasting glucose. Addressing prediabetes meaningfully lowers future CVD risk.
- Kidney health: eGFR ≈69 is acceptable for age; check urine albumin‑to‑creatinine ratio (uACR). CKD requires either eGFR <60 for ≥3 months or albuminuria/other kidney damage markers.
- Weight and lifestyle: BMI 26.1 (overweight). A 5–10% weight loss, DASH/Mediterranean eating pattern, sodium reduction, regular aerobic activity, and limited alcohol all lower BP and ASCVD risk; weight loss also reduces incident AF risk and AF burden if it develops.
- AF screening: Opportunistic pulse checks during visits are reasonable; routine ECG screening in asymptomatic adults has insufficient evidence to improve outcomes. Consider evaluation if symptoms arise or if wearable/home BP devices flag irregular rhythms.

Key numbers (from the calculations above)
- BMI 26.1 kg/m² (Overweight); BSA 1.97 m²
- eGFR (CKD‑EPI): 69.2 mL/min/1.73 m²; Cockcroft–Gault CrCl: 68.8 mL/min
- Corrected Na+: 135.3–135.5 mEq/L (normal)
- Non‑HDL‑C: 170 mg/dL
- 10‑yr ASCVD risk (AHA PREVENT): 10.2% (Intermediate)
- Framingham 10‑yr general CVD risk: ~13.8%
- CHA2DS2‑VASc (if AF present): 1 → ~0.6%/yr ischemic stroke risk

Evidence and sources
- ASCVD risk assessment and statin thresholds: 2018 ACC/AHA Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol. J Am Coll Cardiol. 2019;73:e285–e350. doi:10.1016/j.jacc.2018.11.003
- AHA “PREVENT” risk equations (integrated CVD risk tool incorporating kidney function and treated BP): American Heart Association PREVENT calculator overview and implementation: https://professional.heart.org/en/guidelines-and-statements/prevent-calculator
- Framingham general CVD risk score: D’Agostino RB Sr. et al. General cardiovascular risk profile for use in primary care. Circulation. 2008;117:743–753. doi:10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.107.699579
- Hypertension definitions and treatment targets: 2017 ACC/AHA BP Guideline. J Am Coll Cardiol. 2018;71:e127–e248. doi:10.1016/j.jacc.2017.11.006; SPRINT Research Group. NEJM. 2015;373:2103–2116. doi:10.1056/NEJMoa1511939
- Diabetes/prediabetes diagnostic cutoffs: ADA Standards of Care in Diabetes—2024. Diabetes Care. 2024;47(Suppl 1):S16–S33. doi:10.2337/dc24-S002
- CKD definition: KDIGO 2012 Clinical Practice Guideline. Kidney Int Suppl. 2013;3:1–150. doi:10.1038/kisup.2012.73
- eGFR equation: CKD‑EPI refit (2021). Inker LA et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385:1737–1749. doi:10.1056/NEJMoa2102953
- Cockcroft–Gault formula: Nephron. 1976;16:31–41. doi:10.1159/000180580
- Sodium correction for hyperglycemia: Hillier TA et al. Diabetes Care. 1999;22:1636–1637. doi:10.2337/diacare.22.10.1636
- AF stroke risk (CHA2DS2‑VASc): Olesen JB et al. BMJ. 2011;342:d124. doi:10.1136/bmj.d124; 2023 ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS AF Guideline (for treatment thresholds)
