2025-12-01 11:44:21,319 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Here’s a concise, data-first synthesis of what the most reliable, recent studies show about BRAF mutations in melanoma and how they influence treatment effectiveness and survival. I’ve grouped results by question and cited each claim with the primary paper (PubMed/DOI) so you can verify the numbers.

Key takeaways (with numbers)
- In BRAFV600-mutant metastatic melanoma, starting with dual immune checkpoint blockade (nivolumab + ipilimumab) yields superior survival versus starting with BRAF/MEK targeted therapy. DREAMseq: 2-year overall survival (OS) 72% with first-line nivolumab+ipilimumab vs 52% with first-line dabrafenib+trametinib (P=0.0095; trial stopped early for benefit) [JCO 2023; PMID: 36166727; DOI: 10.1200/JCO.22.01763].
- Long-term outcomes on BRAF/MEK therapy are durable for a subset, but overall 5-year survival is about one-third. Five-year outcomes with dabrafenib+trametinib in metastatic disease: 5-year OS 34% and 5-year PFS 19% overall; in favorable-risk patients (normal LDH and <3 metastatic sites), 5-year OS 55% [NEJM 2019; PMID: 31166680; DOI: 10.1056/NEJMoa1904059].
- Among BRAFV600 subtypes, V600K tends to respond less to BRAF/MEK inhibitors than V600E, but responds at least as well—and often better—to anti–PD-1-based immunotherapy (higher tumor mutational burden/UV signature likely contribute). See direct V600K vs V600E analyses below.

1) Sequencing immunotherapy vs targeted therapy in BRAFV600-mutant metastatic melanoma
- DREAMseq (randomized phase III): First-line nivolumab+ipilimumab vs first-line dabrafenib+trametinib with mandated crossover at progression.
  - 2-year OS: 71.8% (IPI+NIVO first) vs 51.5% (DAB+TRAM first), P=0.0095. Conclusion: immunotherapy-first is superior [PMID: 36166727; DOI: 10.1200/JCO.22.01763].
- SECOMBIT (phase II, 3 arms): immunotherapy first vs targeted therapy first vs an 8-week “sandwich” (short targeted induction then switch to immunotherapy).
  - 4-year follow-up: confirms long-term OS advantage with first-line ipilimumab+nivolumab; “sandwich” approach is a reasonable alternative. The paper also reports exploratory biomarker signals (e.g., JAK loss-of-function, low baseline IFN-γ) [Nature Comms 2024; PMID: 38167503; DOI: 10.1038/s41467-023-44475-6].

Clinical implication: For most BRAFV600-mutant patients, start with dual checkpoint blockade (IPI+NIVO); switch to BRAF/MEK at progression. The “sandwich” approach is a viable alternative if rapid early disease control is needed before transitioning to immunotherapy.

2) Targeted therapy outcomes in BRAFV600-mutant metastatic melanoma (all-comers)
- Dabrafenib+trametinib pooled trials (COMBI-d/v) 5-year update:
  - 5-year OS 34%; 5-year PFS 19%. In favorable-risk patients (normal LDH and <3 metastases), 5-year OS 55% [NEJM 2019; PMID: 31166680; DOI: 10.1056/NEJMoa1904059].
- Encorafenib+binimetinib (COLUMBUS, part 1):
  - Median PFS 14.9 months (vs 7.3 with vemurafenib).
  - Median OS 33.6 months (vs 16.9 with vemurafenib).
  - These are overall BRAFV600E/K results; V600K-specific subgroup numbers were not reported in the abstract [primary COLUMBUS publication; not retrieved in this tool run; see Dummer et al., Lancet Oncol 2018].

3) V600K vs V600E: how mutation subtype affects outcomes
A) With immunotherapy (anti–PD-1 ± anti–CTLA-4)
- Pires da Silva et al., 2019 (multi-institutional cohort; Clinical Cancer Research): V600K tumors had higher tumor mutational burden and superior outcomes to anti–PD-1 vs V600E.
  - Reported differences: higher objective response rate (ORR) and longer PFS for V600K vs V600E on anti–PD-1 therapy; the study concludes V600K is more immunogenic and derives greater benefit from PD-1 blockade [PMID: 30630828; DOI: 10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-18-1680].
  - Note: The abstract page doesn’t list the precise ORR/PFS numbers; they are detailed in the full text. The directionality (V600K > V600E for PD-1 outcomes) is consistent across independent analyses and clinical experience.

B) With BRAF±MEK inhibition
- Vemurafenib (BRIM-3 extended follow-up): Both V600E and V600K benefit over dacarbazine, but V600K outcomes are lower than V600E.
  - Extended follow-up confirms improved OS and PFS vs dacarbazine in V600K, though with lower ORR and shorter PFS/OS than V600E [PMID: 24508103; DOI: 10.1016/S1470-2045(14)70012-9].
  - Exact V600K subgroup medians/ORR are in the paper’s subgroup tables; directionality is clear in the abstract and aligns with subsequent pooled analyses.
- Clinical context: Reviews and pooled analyses consistently report that V600K has lower response and shorter durability to BRAF/MEK than V600E, reinforcing the preference for immunotherapy-first in V600K when clinically feasible [e.g., Haugh & Johnson, 2019, review; PMID: 31741065].

Practical conclusion for V600K: If the patient is not acutely symptomatic (i.e., no urgent need for rapid cytoreduction), immunotherapy-first is typically favored. Targeted therapy remains highly active and is appropriate when immediate disease control is needed or after immunotherapy.

4) Non-V600 BRAF (class II/class III) melanoma
- MEK inhibitor monotherapy has meaningful but modest activity in selected non-V600 alterations; immunotherapy generally remains first-line.
- Trametinib phase II in non-V600 BRAF-mutant and BRAF fusion melanoma (Nebhan et al., 2021, The Oncologist):
  - Study supports clinical activity of trametinib in non-V600 cases; details by mutation class are in the full text (small cohort).
  - The authors recommend sequencing to find noncanonical BRAF mutations that may respond to MEK inhibition [PMID: 33861486; DOI: 10.1002/onco.13795].
- Contemporary expert reviews emphasize:
  - Class II (kinase-activated dimers, e.g., K601E, L597) can be MEK-sensitive.
  - Class III (kinase-impaired, RAS-dependent) often do better with immunotherapy; targeted approaches may require combinations (e.g., pan-RAF + MEK). Evidence is largely preclinical or from small series.

5) Adjuvant setting (resected stage III, BRAFV600)
- Dabrafenib+trametinib (COMBI-AD):
  - 5-year analysis: significantly improves relapse-free survival (RFS) vs placebo (hazard ratio [HR] for relapse or death ≈0.47 in the 2020 interim; final 2024 NEJM paper confirms sustained RFS and distant metastasis–free survival benefits) [NEJM 2020: PMID: 32877599; NEJM 2024 final: PMID: 38899716; DOI: 10.1056/NEJMoa2404139].
- Practice guidelines shifted to checkpoint inhibitors and BRAF/MEK inhibitors as preferred adjuvant options for high-risk resected melanoma (replacing interferon) [Ontario Health/CCO guideline, 2020; PMID: 32218667].
- MRD/ctDNA: In 2025, ddPCR-based BRAF-mutant ctDNA assays in stage III adjuvant setting predicted survival and stratified recurrence risk, supporting ctDNA-guided risk adaptation [Lancet Oncology 2025; PMID: 40250457/40449497 series; DOI: 10.1016/S1470-2045(25)00139-1].

6) Triplet strategies (IO + BRAF + MEK) in metastatic BRAFV600 melanoma
- COMBI-i (spartalizumab + dabrafenib + trametinib) did not improve PFS over placebo + dabrafenib + trametinib [JITC 2022; DOI: 10.1136/jitc-2021-004226].
- IMspire150 (atezolizumab + vemurafenib + cobimetinib) met its PFS endpoint in that trial, but across the totality of data, OS/PFS advantages for triplets have been inconsistent; 2023 commentary emphasizes longer duration of response without consistent OS benefit and the need to weigh toxicity [2023 review; PMID: 37543586].

7) Prognostic impact of BRAF mutation (overall)
- Systematic review/meta-analysis (2020): BRAF mutation status is associated with worse OS vs BRAF wild-type in melanoma (pooled HR >1). Exact pooled HR and subgroup analyses are detailed in the paper [PMID: 32285732; DOI: 10.1080/0284186X.2020.1747636].

What this means for your project
- BRAFV600E vs V600K:
  - Targeted therapy: V600K generally has lower ORR and shorter PFS/OS than V600E with BRAF±MEK inhibitors (e.g., BRIM-3 subgroup; confirm values in tables) [PMID: 24508103].
  - Immunotherapy: V600K shows higher tumor mutational burden and better outcomes on anti–PD-1 than V600E (higher ORR, longer PFS reported) [PMID: 30630828].
  - Net effect: For V600K, immunotherapy-first is especially compelling; targeted therapy remains crucial for rapid control or post-IO sequencing.
- Sequencing (V600E/K combined): DREAMseq and SECOMBIT support immunotherapy-first for durable OS; a brief targeted “induction” then switch (SECOMBIT “sandwich”) is reasonable when initial debulking is needed [PMIDs: 36166727; 38167503].
- Non-V600 (class II/III): Small but growing evidence supports selective MEK inhibitor activity (greater for class II) and the need for trials of rational combinations (e.g., pan-RAF + MEK). Immunotherapy remains the preferred first-line in most cases [PMID: 33861486].

Citations (primary sources to verify numbers)
- DREAMseq (first-line sequencing): Atkins et al., JCO 2023. 2-yr OS 72% (IO-first) vs 52% (TT-first) [PMID: 36166727; DOI: 10.1200/JCO.22.01763].
- SECOMBIT (4-year): Ascierto et al., Nat Commun 2024. IO-first durable OS advantage; “sandwich” feasible [PMID: 38167503; DOI: 10.1038/s41467-023-44475-6].
- Dabrafenib+trametinib in metastatic melanoma (5-year): Robert et al., NEJM 2019. 5-yr OS 34%, 5-yr PFS 19%; OS 55% in favorable baseline [PMID: 31166680; DOI: 10.1056/NEJMoa1904059].
- Vemurafenib vs dacarbazine (BRIM-3; V600K subgroup reported): McArthur et al., Lancet Oncol 2014. V600K benefits from vemurafenib but with lower ORR/PFS/OS vs V600E [PMID: 24508103; DOI: 10.1016/S1470-2045(14)70012-9].
- V600K vs V600E on immunotherapy: Pires da Silva et al., Clin Cancer Res 2019. V600K has higher TMB and better anti–PD-1 outcomes than V600E [PMID: 30630828; DOI: 10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-18-1680].
- Non-V600 BRAF (trametinib): Nebhan et al., The Oncologist 2021. Activity in selected non-V600/fusion melanomas; sequencing recommended [PMID: 33861486; DOI: 10.1002/onco.13795].
- Adjuvant dabrafenib+trametinib (COMBI-AD): Dummer et al., NEJM 2020 5-yr analysis [PMID: 32877599]; Long et al., NEJM 2024 final results (sustained RFS/DMFS benefit) [PMID: 38899716; DOI: 10.1056/NEJMoa2404139].
- Triplet therapy commentary: 2023 review summarizing IMspire150 and others [PMID: 37543586].
- Prognostic meta-analysis of BRAF status: Ny et al., 2020 [PMID: 32285732; DOI: 10.1080/0284186X.2020.1747636].

If you need, I can extract the exact V600K subgroup numbers (e.g., BRIM-3 V600K ORR/PFS/OS medians; the PD-1 ORR and PFS medians in Pires da Silva 2019) from the full texts for tables/figures in your project.
