Paper 2 (PMID: 38167503; 2024; SECOMBIT; NCT02631447; DOI: 10.1038/s41467-023-44475-6)
- Design: Randomized, three-arm, noncomparative phase II trial in BRAFV600-mutant metastatic melanoma comparing:
  - Arm A: First-line immunotherapy (ipilimumab + nivolumab) until progression, then BRAF/MEK (encorafenib + binimetinib).
  - Arm B: First-line BRAF/MEK, then immunotherapy at progression.
  - Arm C (“sandwich”): 8-week induction with BRAF/MEK → planned switch to immunotherapy until progression → BRAF/MEK again.
- Previously reported primary outcome: Overall survival favored first-line immunotherapy (ipi+nivo) administered until progression, followed by BRAF/MEK.
- New 4-year results: Confirm durable, long-term benefit with first-line immunotherapy for most patients with BRAFV600-mutant metastatic melanoma.
- Biomarkers (predefined, exploratory):
  - A trend toward improved 4-year overall survival and total progression-free survival in patients with JAK loss-of-function mutations.
  - A similar trend in patients with low baseline serum interferon-gamma (IFNγ).
