Overall themes
- Targeted therapy (BRAF/MEK inhibition) and immunotherapy are both central in BRAF V600-mutant melanoma. Choice and sequencing are active questions; biomarkers (MAPK activation, ctDNA) and early imaging can guide decisions.
- Adjuvant therapy with dabrafenib plus trametinib improves relapse-free outcomes in resected stage III BRAF V600 melanoma, with supportive patient-reported outcomes. Real-world adjuvant data are emerging.
- Liquid biopsy (ctDNA) is increasingly validated for prognosis, minimal residual disease, and monitoring; methodology (ddPCR, WGS-derived CNI) matters.
- Non-V600 and V600K BRAF mutations show distinct biology/behavior, with limited but growing data on optimal treatment.
- Management nuances include AE prevention/management for encorafenib+binimetinib, handling rare hypersensitivity to dabrafenib, and surgical de-escalation (avoiding routine CLND).

