Adjuvant therapy evidence and guidance
- COMBI-AD (5-year analysis; NEJM 2020): Twelve months of adjuvant dabrafenib plus trametinib (DabTram) in resected stage III BRAF-mutant melanoma produced significantly longer relapse-free survival than placebo, with sustained benefit at 5 years.
- Lancet Oncology 2020 analysis: Adjuvant DabTram reduced risk of relapse versus placebo in resected BRAF-mutant stage III melanoma.
- Ontario Health (Cancer Care Ontario) guideline update (2020): Replaces interferon with immune checkpoint inhibitors and targeted therapies (e.g., DabTram) as preferred systemic adjuvant options for high-risk resected cutaneous or mucosal melanoma.
- Review (2025): Adjuvant therapy significantly reduces recurrence risk in melanoma; selecting the optimal regimen can be challenging and must be individualized.
- Real-world cohorts: Italian MAP-MADAM (2024 interim analysis) and additional high-risk, adjuvant-treated cohorts (2023) are defining patient characteristics and outcomes outside trials.

V600K-specific insights and cases
- Two cases (2025): BRAF V600K melanoma recurred during adjuvant DabTram, highlighting potential limitations and the need for alternative strategies or biomarkers in V600K.
- Case (2022): Metastatic melanoma with rare BRAF V600K mutation responded to DabTram.
- PET-guided sequencing case (2020): A V600K patient with progression on pembrolizumab at 3 months switched promptly to BRAF/MEK targeted therapy, achieving near-complete metabolic response by 3 months and durable response at 10 months.
- Leptomeningeal disease (LMD) case (2025): A 69-year-old man with BRAF V600K melanoma developed LMD during a treatment pause. Reintroduction of encorafenib plus binimetinib at reduced doses led to rapid radiologic improvement within days and subsequent disease response, with survival ~14 months from LMD diagnosis—reported as the first such rapid radiologic response and prolonged survival with this regimen in melanoma LMD.

Biomarkers and minimal residual disease
- Lancet Oncology 2025 (ddPCR ctDNA): Clinical validation of droplet digital PCR assays detecting BRAF ctDNA in stage III melanoma showed ctDNA measurements during adjuvant targeted therapy or placebo predict survival outcomes and stratify recurrence risk, supporting ctDNA as a tool for risk-adapted adjuvant management.

Combinations and novel strategies
- COMBI-i phase 3 (2022): Adding anti–PD-1 spartalizumab to DabTram did not improve progression-free survival versus placebo plus DabTram in unresectable/metastatic BRAF-mutant melanoma.
- Phase 1 triple-targeted trial (2024): Evaluated combined BRAF, MEK, and AKT inhibition to counter PI3K/AKT-mediated resistance in BRAF-mutated solid tumors; explored feasibility and safety of dual MAPK and PI3K pathway blockade.

Special molecular and clinical scenarios
- BRAF fusion: A 71-year-old woman with melanoma harboring a rare BRAF-ZKSCAN5 fusion showed tumor shrinkage (22.2% by length at 1 month) on DabTram but discontinued after 6 weeks due to grade 3 nausea, fatigue, and diarrhea; indicates potential sensitivity of BRAF fusions to BRAF/MEK inhibition with attention to tolerability.
- Primary pulmonary melanoma (2020): Managed with surgery followed by DabTram, illustrating targeted therapy utility in rare primary sites.
- Mediastinal metastatic melanoma (2020): Recurrent melanoma presenting as a large mediastinal mass; treated with pembrolizumab while BRAF testing was pending, underscoring diagnostic and therapeutic complexities.

