Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'non-V600 BRAF melanoma MEK inhibitor outcomes', 'max_results': 20} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 2834 tokens): Summary of key evidence on targeted therapy for non-V600 (rare) BRAF-mutated melanoma

Context and need
- Standard of care: In BRAF V600E/K melanoma, combined BRAF+MEK inhibition is well established with high response rates (up to ~70%). However, efficacy in advanced melanoma with rare/non-V600 BRAF mutations remains uncertain.
- Prevalence and biology: Approximately 35% of all BRAF mutations across cancers are non-V600; in melanoma, about 7% involve BRAF mutations beyond codon 600. Many non-V600 variants are oncogenic and fall into three functional classes; class 2 (kinase-activated, RAS-independent dimers) and class 3 (kinase-impaired, RAS-dependent) lack consensus treatment strategies.
- Clinical implication: Non-V600 BRAF-mutant melanomas show heterogeneous kinase activity and signaling dependence, which influence sensitivity to MAPK-targeted therapies. Overall, outcomes with currently available targeted therapies in non-V600 disease are typically inferior to V600, so immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI) remain the recommended first-line therapy for most patients without BRAF V600 mutations. Nevertheless, MEK inhibition can benefit select non-V600 variants.

Updated multicenter analysis and global database (Menzer et al., EJCA 2025)
- An international data collection has been updated, adding 49 new patients with rare BRAF-mutated melanoma treated with targeted therapy.
- A publicly accessible global outcome database has been launched to systematically capture real-world efficacy and inform practice for these rare genotypes.

Single-institution cohort of non-V600 E/K BRAF melanoma (Comito et al., 2022)
- Retrospective study of 14 stage III/IV melanoma patients (2011–2021) with non-V600E/K BRAF mutations: V600R, V600_K601delinsE, K601E, p.T599_V600insT, L597V, G466R, S467L, A598T.
- Notably, G466R and A598T BRAF mutations had not been previously reported in melanoma.
- Treatments: 4 patients received combined BRAF/MEK inhibitors, 2 received BRAF inhibitor monotherapy, 6 received ICI for advanced disease; 4 received adjuvant nivolumab.
- Given small numbers and lack of randomized trials, detailed reporting is emphasized to guide care.

MAPK-targeted therapy across non-V600 BRAF tumors (Dankner et al., 2022)
- Non-V600 mutations comprise ~35% of BRAF mutations in cancer and include multiple oncogenic drivers.
- Highlights classification into three molecular classes and the absence of consensus strategies for class 2/3 mutations.
- Underscores the need for tailored approaches and prospective data.

Therapeutic framework for non-V600 BRAF melanoma (Menzer & Hassel, 2022 review)
- Suggests classifying BRAF mutations by kinase activation and signaling dependencies to guide therapy.
- Non-V600 tumors often require RAS signaling; MEK inhibition may be more beneficial than BRAF inhibition in such cases.
- Other driver alterations (e.g., KIT, NRAS) may be actionable in selected melanomas.
- Because responses and durability to targeted agents in non-V600 melanoma are generally inferior to V600, ICI remain first-line; targeted therapy may be considered in selected cases or lines.

Detection and profiling (Boussemart et al., 2019)
- Hybrid capture-based comprehensive genomic profiling can identify both V600 and non-V600 BRAF alterations in samples previously negative by other tests.
- Given observed responses to targeted therapies in diverse non-V600 alterations, sensitive, broad testing is critical to optimize patient-treatment matching.

Broader targeted therapy landscape (Iams et al., 2017 review)
- Confirms superiority of combined BRAF+MEK over monotherapy in V600 melanoma and outlines resistance strategies under study.
- For non-V600 melanoma, MEK inhibitor monotherapy is a potential targeted option.
- Parallel mutation-specific approaches: NRAS (MEK ± CDK4/6 inhibitors), KIT (TKIs), NF1 (mTOR and MEK inhibitors). Emerging agents in related settings (e.g., uveal melanoma) noted.

