Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'class II BRAF melanoma outcomes', 'max_results': 20} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 6230 tokens): - Targeting BRAF Class II/III in NSCLC with exarafenib (pan-RAF inhibitor)
  - Context: Class II/III BRAF mutations comprise ~65% of BRAF-mutant NSCLC; Class II associated with worse outcomes vs Class I.
  - Findings: Exarafenib (type 2 pan-RAF, active regardless of RAF isoform/dimerization) showed robust anti-tumor activity in multiple preclinical Class II/III BRAF models; initial clinical signals of activity in Class II BRAF–mutant NSCLC.
  - Resistance mechanism: Drug-induced adaptive rewiring to an ARAF-mediated bypass via ARAF–KSR1 scaffolding complexes that preserve MAPK signaling under therapy; driven by upstream RTK activation and RAS-GTP accumulation that promotes complex assembly during drug exposure.
  - Strategy: MEK inhibition (e.g., binimetinib) targets the convergence point to overcome resistance. Exarafenib + binimetinib outperformed monotherapy across diverse preclinical models.
  - Implication: Establishes ARAF–KSR1 complexes as a novel resistance mechanism to pan-RAF inhibition and supports rational combinations for Class II/III BRAF-mutant NSCLC.

- Rac1-driven melanoma resistance to MAPK inhibition and FAK dependency (Oncogene 2025; preprint 2024)
  - Context: Rac1 P29S mutation and Rac1 GEF activation associate with MAPK inhibitor (MAPKi) resistance, especially in undifferentiated melanoma, and with worse outcomes.
  - Mechanisms: 
    - Reduced dependence on BRAF/MEK (but not complete ERK independence).
    - Activation of alternative MAPKs (JNK, p38).
    - Partial reliance on YAP/TAZ.
    - Creation of a dependency on focal adhesion kinase (FAK) signaling in undifferentiated cells.
  - Therapeutic strategy: Combined RAF/MEK clamp avutometinib (VS-6766; “auvotometinib” in preprint) plus FAK inhibitor defactinib suppresses growth of Rac1-driven melanoma; may be useful for MAPKi-resistant, heavily pretreated disease and is under investigation in brain metastatic melanoma.

- Melanoma progress and outlook (CCR 2025 “Facts and Hopes” review)
  - Outcomes: Stage IV melanoma death rates decreased 3–5% annually over the past decade due to targeted therapy (BRAF/MEK inhibitors) and immunotherapy.
  - Approved modalities: Checkpoint inhibitors (CTLA-4, PD-1, LAG-3), T-cell engager targeting gp100, and TIL therapy enable durable responses in up to ~50% of advanced cases; adjuvant/neoadjuvant use reduces relapse in stage II–III.
  - Needs: Refractory/nonresponsive disease and rare subtypes (mucosal, acral, uveal) remain challenging.
  - Future directions: Modified T-cell approaches (TCR, CAR-T), CRISPR editing; deeper understanding of tumor/immune microenvironment, heterogeneity, microbiome; prevention and resistance mechanisms to further lower mortality.

