  - Approach: Hybrid capture of incompletely spliced, chromatin-associated pre-mRNAs; deep sequencing of NF-kB transcriptome after transcriptional freeze to measure splicing kinetics.
  - Key findings: Many introns splice tens to hundreds of times slower than average. Conserved introns with suboptimal donor sites act as timing elements; strengthening donor sites by as few as two nucleotides in minigene reporters markedly increases expression and reduces delays.
  - Additional mechanisms: Deep learning sequence-to-function models with feature attribution identified intronic and exonic cis-elements, including non-canonical motifs, enriched in slow-splicing introns, indicating donor strength–independent contributors to delayed splicing.
  - Implication: Temporal regulation of pre-mRNA splicing tunes onset and amplitude of inflammatory gene expression, likely generalizable to other rapid-response programs.

- Ddx3x regulates B-cell development and immunoglobulin rearrangement in mice (immunology)
  - Genetic models: Conditional deletion in developing B cells (Mb1-Cre females: homozygous; Vav1-Cre or Mb1-Cre males: hemizygous).
  - Phenotypes: Females showed complete loss of mature peripheral B cells with a block at the pro-B stage. Males had milder reductions and marginal zone skewing, suggesting partial compensation (e.g., Ddx3y).
  - Mechanism: Loss of Ddx3x in pre-B cells reduced Brwd1 expression, failed to curtail proliferation, and impaired Igk rearrangement, skewing light chain usage toward lambda.
  - Conclusion: Ddx3x is essential for cell-cycle control and epigenetic regulation enabling immunoglobulin gene rearrangement and normal B-cell development.

- Loss of ErbB3 redirects Integrin β1 from early endosomal recycling to secretion in extracellular vesicles (cell biology)
  - Findings: ErbB3 sorts internalized integrin β1 and transferrin receptor for recycling (likely via Rab4-positive compartment) independently of ligand-induced signaling. ErbB3 loss blocks recycling, redirects integrin β1 to lysosomal degradation and EV secretion.
  - Consequences: Reduced surface integrin β1, impaired collective migration; EVs from ErbB3-depleted cells increase motility of wild-type cells.
  - Mechanism: ErbB3 promotes assembly of the Arf6-GGA3-Rabaptin5 endosomal sorting complex, facilitating early recycling and suppressing EV release.
  - Implication: Pseudo-RTKs actively regulate endosomal sorting and MVB/EV biology.

