 FINAL SOLUTION:
2025-12-01 12:14:43,769 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Executive summary
- There were four geomagnetic storm intervals in early–mid November 2025, driven by multiple CMEs. The most significant was 12 Nov 2025, peaking at Kp 8.67 (NOAA G4 “Severe” range), with the magnetopause compressed inside geosynchronous orbit and follow-on radiation belt enhancements. All of these assertions are backed by NASA/CCMC DONKI event records (links below).
- Late‑November CMEs (25–29 Nov) were mostly limb/far‑sided. WSA–Enlil model runs show impacts to spacecraft (e.g., Parker Solar Probe, Mars) but no clear Earth-directed impacts in that window, implying lower near‑term risk to Earth from those particular eruptions.
- Who/where is affected: High‑latitude regions (Alaska/Canada/Scandinavia/Russia/New Zealand’s South Island) see the primary impacts in G2–G3 storms; mid‑latitudes can be affected in G4 events like 12 Nov (aurora and potential grid/HF/GNSS effects). For impact definitions and thresholds, see NOAA SWPC’s Geomagnetic Storm Scale (G1–G5).

Recent, verifiable examples with data
1) 12 Nov 2025 (major event; two storm intervals)
- 00:00Z storm interval: Kp 8.67 at 03:00Z and 8.33 at 06:00Z (G4 Severe) 
  Source/evidence:
  • DONKI Geomagnetic Storm: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42642/-1
  • Related CMEs listed by DONKI: 7 Nov 12:53Z, 9 Nov 07:24Z, 10 Nov 09:36Z; interplanetary shock at 11 Nov 22:11Z; magnetopause crossing at 12 Nov 00:59Z indicating compression inside GEO: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/MPC/42637/-1
  • Radiation belt enhancement on 13 Nov 14:25Z following those CMEs: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/RBE/42674/-1
- 18:00Z storm interval: Kp 7.33 at 13 Nov 03:00Z (G3 Strong)
  Source/evidence:
  • DONKI Geomagnetic Storm: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42662/-1
  • Related CMEs: 10 Nov 13:08Z, 11 Nov 10:23Z; interplanetary shock 12 Nov 18:52Z; magnetopause crossing 13 Nov 01:27Z: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/MPC/42669/-1

2) 8 Nov 2025 (strong storm)
- Start: 00:00Z; Kp 6.33 at 03:00Z (G2–G3 level impacts likely at times)
  Source/evidence:
  • DONKI Geomagnetic Storm: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42533/-1
  • Related CME: 5 Nov 22:23Z; interplanetary shock 7 Nov 21:54Z.

3) 5–6 Nov 2025 (strong storm)
- Start: 5 Nov 21:00Z; Kp 6.0 at 6 Nov 00:00Z and 6.67 at 06:00Z (G2–G3 level)
  Source/evidence:
  • DONKI Geomagnetic Storm: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42455/-1
  • Related CME: 3 Nov 09:36Z; interplanetary shock 5 Nov 16:50Z; magnetopause crossing 6 Nov 03:51Z: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/MPC/42473/-1

CME activity and speeds (selected, recent, with links)
- Early Nov (Earth-impacting group contributing to the 5–13 Nov storms)
  • 3 Nov 04:36Z CME associated with a C7.1 flare (AR 14274), speed ~686 km/s: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/42347/-1
  • 3 Nov 09:36Z CME associated with M1.6 flare, linked to the 5–6 Nov storm: (see GST 42455 above). 
- Late Nov (mostly far‑sided/limb; lower Earth risk)
  • 28 Nov 06:24Z CME tied to a C5.1 flare beyond the east limb; speed ~833 km/s: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43069/-1 and flare https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/FLR/ — entry 2025-11-28T05:43:00-FLR-001
  • 28 Nov 16:36Z fast CME, speed ~1049 km/s (overlapping earlier limb event): https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43081/-1
  • 28 Nov 23:45Z CME from near-limb AR 14294 after an M5.9 flare; speed ~938 km/s: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43090/-1 and flare 2025-11-28T22:11Z (M5.9)
  • 25 Nov series (multiple NE/NNE events, many far‑sided), speeds from ~297 to 1128 km/s; example fast event 25 Nov 02:48Z at 1128 km/s: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/42989/-1

Solar energetic particles (SEPs) tied to those CMEs/flares
- SEP onsets on 10–11 Nov (multiple entries), associated with 10 Nov X1.2 flare and CMEs, and 11 Nov activity. These raise radiation risk for polar aviation and satellites:
  • Example SEP entries (10 Nov 10:24Z–15:04Z, 11 Nov 09:45Z–11:09Z): https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/SEP/42581/-1 (and adjacent 42579/42580/42582/42585/etc.)

Magnetospheric effects confirming storm severity
- Magnetopause crossings (compression inside geosynchronous orbit):
  • 6 Nov 03:51Z: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/MPC/42473/-1
  • 12 Nov 00:59Z: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/MPC/42637/-1
  • 13 Nov 01:27Z: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/MPC/42669/-1
- Radiation belt enhancements (impacting satellite radiation environment):
  • 8 Nov 15:15Z (linked to preceding CME+HSS): https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/RBE/42537/-1
  • 13 Nov 14:25Z (linked to 9–11 Nov CMEs): https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/RBE/42674/-1

What this means for “areas affected”
- Geomagnetic storms preferentially impact high‑latitude regions; the footprint expands equatorward with larger Kp/G levels.
  • For Kp≈6–7 (G2–G3): aurora often extends into southern Canada/northern tier of the U.S., Scotland/Scandinavia, northern Germany/Poland/Baltics, southern New Zealand; power systems can see elevated reactive power and alarm rates at high latitudes; HF and GNSS can have degradations at high latitudes.
  • For Kp≥8 (G4, as on 12 Nov): mid‑latitudes can be affected; aurora can reach much farther south (e.g., central U.S./Europe), and power system voltage control issues and potential protective device operations are possible at high latitudes; GNSS position errors and HF comms disruptions are more likely.
  • NOAA SWPC impact definitions and thresholds: https://www.swpc.noaa.gov/noaa-scales-explanation
- Aviation and HF/VHF users at polar routes: The 10–11 Nov SEP activity indicates elevated radiation/absorption risks at high latitudes during/after those events (S‑scale events are commonly assessed by SWPC); see NOAA scales for radiation storms: https://www.swpc.noaa.gov/noaa-scales-explanation
- Satellites: Evidence of magnetopause compression (MPC links above) and radiation belt enhancements (RBE links) indicate increased satellite charging, single‑event upset, and anomaly risk around 6–13 Nov 2025.

Near‑term outlook based on late‑November data
- Most CMEs from 25–29 Nov were on/behind the east/northeast limb (multiple DONKI CME analyses cite “far‑sided/behind limb”), and WSA–Enlil runs around 28–30 Nov show impacts to Parker Solar Probe and Mars, not specifically to Earth:
  • Example model with impacts to PSP (29–30 Nov) and Mars (2 Dec): WSA-ENLIL/43080/1 — summary: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/WSA-ENLIL/43080/1
- A high‑speed stream (HSS) arrived 28 Nov 14:57Z: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/HSS/43094/-1
  • HSS can cause G1–G2-type activity; however, there is no DONKI record of Earth geomagnetic storms in the 25 Nov–1 Dec window comparable to 12 Nov. For an operational forecast of the next 3 days, consult NOAA SWPC: https://www.swpc.noaa.gov/products/3-day-forecast

Quantifying the November 2025 storminess (at-a-glance)
- Number of DONKI geomagnetic storm intervals logged: 4 (5–13 Nov 2025), with peak Kp values:
  • 6 Nov: Kp 6.67 (G3) — https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42455/-1
  • 8 Nov: Kp 6.33 (G2–G3) — https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42533/-1
  • 12 Nov (00Z): Kp 8.67 and 8.33 (G4) — https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42642/-1
  • 12–13 Nov (18Z): up to Kp 7.33 (G3) — https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42662/-1
- CME plane‑of‑sky speeds in the late‑Nov limb series ranged ~297–1128 km/s (examples above).

What to tell your boss (evidence-backed)
- Yes—recent CMEs have produced geomagnetic storms affecting Earth. The most serious in this period was 12 Nov 2025 (Kp 8.67; G4 Severe) with documented magnetopause compression (00:59Z MPC) and follow‑on radiation belt enhancements (13 Nov). Verified via NASA/CCMC DONKI records linked above.
- Impacts at G3–G4 levels include high‑latitude grid voltage control issues, potential transformer heating, degraded HF radio/GNSS accuracy, satellite anomaly risk, and aurora extending to mid‑latitudes. Impact definitions and thresholds are per NOAA SWPC’s scales (link above).
- Current risk from the late‑November CMEs appears lower for Earth because most eruptions were limb/far‑sided; model runs show impacts to spacecraft and Mars rather than Earth. A coronal high‑speed stream on 28 Nov may still produce modest activity; check SWPC’s 3‑day forecast for the latest.

Sources and further reading (primary, verifiable)
