2025-12-01 12:42:07,835 - benchmark.runner - INFO - What’s being compared
- GitHub v3 REST API (for managing repositories and much more). Base: https://api.github.com. Official docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/
- OpenAI REST API (for models, responses, files, vector stores, assistants, etc.). Base: https://api.openai.com/v1. Docs: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference

Executive takeaways (with evidence)
- Scope/coverage: GitHub exposes repository management and related operations across 1,108 REST endpoints (source: OpenAPI summary, https://oapis.org/summary/github) and includes repo lifecycle, collaborators, contents, commits, branches, and branch protection (examples and docs cited below). OpenAI’s API does not manage code repositories; it focuses on AI model operations (models, chat/responses, files, vector stores, assistants, etc.). Its spec lists ApiKeyAuth and groups such as Responses, Chat, Files, Vector stores, Assistants, Realtime, etc. (OpenAPI v2.3.0; base URL and groups in: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference; see also OpenAPI spec metadata returned for /v1/models, /v1/chat/completions, /v1/files, /v1/vector_stores, /v1/assistants, /v1/responses).
- Authentication: GitHub supports multiple auth modes; for GitHub Apps, you create short‑lived installation access tokens that expire after 1 hour (POST /app/installations/{installation_id}/access_tokens) and can scope to selected repositories and permissions (docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/apps/apps#create-an-installation-access-token-for-an-app). OpenAI uses API keys via Authorization: Bearer over HTTPS (OpenAPI Security: ApiKeyAuth; docs: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/introduction and https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/authentication).
- Webhooks: GitHub sends HMAC‑signed webhook deliveries with X-Hub-Signature-256 (e.g., branch_protection_configuration event; docs: https://docs.github.com/webhooks/webhook-events-and-payloads#branch_protection_configuration). OpenAI also offers webhooks for events such as response completed, batch completed, fine-tuning job updates, etc. (docs: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/webhooks).

How repository operations compare
- GitHub: provides full repository CRUD and admin surface (selected, with exact routes and parameters from the REST docs/spec):
  - List repos for the authenticated user: GET /user/repos with parameters visibility ∈ {all, public, private}, type ∈ {all, owner, public, private, member}, affiliation CSV, sort ∈ {created, updated, pushed, full_name}, direction ∈ {asc, desc}, per_page (default 30, max 100), page, since/before (ISO 8601). You cannot use type together with visibility or affiliation; doing so returns 422 (docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#list-repositories-for-the-authenticated-user; OpenAPI opId repos/list-for-authenticated-user).
  - Create repo (for the authenticated user): POST /user/repos (docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#create-a-repository-for-the-authenticated-user).
  - List org repos: GET /orgs/{org}/repos (docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#list-organization-repositories).
  - Delete a repo: DELETE /repos/{owner}/{repo} (docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#delete-a-repository).
  - Add a collaborator: PUT /repos/{owner}/{repo}/collaborators/{username} (docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/collaborators/collaborators#add-a-repository-collaborator).
  - Get branches: GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/branches (docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/branches/branches#list-branches).
  - Get file/dir contents: GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/contents/{path} (docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/contents#get-repository-content).
  - List commits: GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/commits (docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/commits/commits#list-commits).
  - Branch protection (read/update): see https://docs.github.com/rest/branches/branch-protection
- OpenAI: no repository CRUD. The closest adjacent features are:
  - Files API (/v1/files) for uploading and managing files used by models (not version control; docs: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/files).
  - Vector stores (/v1/vector_stores) for retrieval-augmented workflows (docs: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/vector-stores).
  - Assistants, Responses, Chat Completions, etc. for model execution, not repo management (docs: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/assistants, https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/responses, https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/chat).

Authentication details (differences with concrete data)
- GitHub
  - GitHub App installation tokens: POST /app/installations/{installation_id}/access_tokens; tokens expire 1 hour after creation (401 on expiry). You can restrict the token to up to 500 specific repositories by name or ID and optionally restrict permissions in the request body; the token cannot exceed the app’s granted permissions or repositories (docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/apps/apps#create-an-installation-access-token-for-an-app).
  - Webhook signatures: deliveries include X-Hub-Signature-256 (sha256=...) for HMAC verification (docs: event payloads page above).
  - Base URL and OpenAPI info: https://api.github.com; OpenAPI 3.0.3; REST API version 1.1.4 (OpenAPI metadata).
- OpenAI
  - API key security: scheme ApiKeyAuth; send Authorization: Bearer YOUR_KEY over HTTPS to https://api.openai.com/v1 (OpenAPI for /v1/models, /v1/chat/completions, /v1/files, /v1/assistants, /v1/responses show Security: ApiKeyAuth; docs: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/authentication).
  - Webhooks: supports events like response completed/failed, batch completed/failed, fine-tuning job updates, evals, realtime call incoming (docs: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/webhooks).

Documentation quality and specificity (evidence)
- GitHub
  - Parameter constraints are explicit. Example: GET /user/repos documents that per_page defaults to 30 and max is 100, and that type cannot be used with visibility or affiliation (422 if combined). Docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#list-repositories-for-the-authenticated-user
  - Webhook event schemas include header names and payload schemas (e.g., branch_protection_configuration with action=disabled, and signature header X-Hub-Signature-256). Docs: https://docs.github.com/webhooks/webhook-events-and-payloads#branch_protection_configuration
  - The Apps token creation docs specify time-bound tokens (1 hour), scoping to selected repositories (max 500), and granular permissions (docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/apps/apps#create-an-installation-access-token-for-an-app).
- OpenAI
  - The OpenAPI spec groups endpoints clearly (Responses, Chat, Files, Vector stores, Assistants, Realtime) and declares ApiKeyAuth and base URL https://api.openai.com/v1 (OpenAPI v2.3.0; e.g., /v1/models, /v1/chat/completions, /v1/files, /v1/vector_stores, /v1/assistants, /v1/responses).
  - Webhooks and Streaming events are explicitly enumerated (e.g., ResponseCompleted, BatchCompleted webhooks; extensive SSE event list for Responses). Docs: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/webhooks and https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/responses

Security best practices you can apply immediately (grounded in the docs)
- GitHub
  - Prefer GitHub Apps over user tokens; use short‑lived installation tokens that expire after 1 hour (docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/apps/apps#create-an-installation-access-token-for-an-app).
  - Scope tokens to only the repositories you need (up to 500 repos per token request) and to the minimal permissions required (same doc).
  - Verify webhook signatures using X-Hub-Signature-256 with your shared secret before trusting events (docs: https://docs.github.com/webhooks/webhook-events-and-payloads).
  - Use pagination and respect per_page limits (max 100) to avoid data leakage and reduce blast radius (docs for GET /user/repos).
- OpenAI
  - Store API keys securely; send only via HTTPS with Authorization: Bearer (docs: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/authentication).
  - Use separate keys per environment/project, rotate keys regularly, and apply least-privilege at the organizational/project level per OpenAI account policies.
  - If you use OpenAI webhooks, verify signatures/secret per the webhook guide (docs: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/webhooks).

Concrete examples (requests)
- GitHub: list repositories for the authenticated user
  - curl -H "Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN" "https://api.github.com/user/repos?per_page=100&visibility=private"
  - Docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#list-repositories-for-the-authenticated-user
- GitHub: create an installation access token (scope to selected repos and permissions)
  - POST https://api.github.com/app/installations/{installation_id}/access_tokens with JSON body like:
    - { "repository_ids": [12345, 67890], "permissions": { "contents": "read", "issues": "write" } }
  - Docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/apps/apps#create-an-installation-access-token-for-an-app
  - Facts: token expires in 1 hour; cannot exceed the app’s granted permissions; up to 500 repositories can be selected (same doc).
- GitHub: delete a repository
  - curl -X DELETE -H "Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN" "https://api.github.com/repos/{owner}/{repo}"
  - Docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#delete-a-repository
- OpenAI: list models
  - curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY" https://api.openai.com/v1/models
  - Spec metadata confirms base URL and ApiKeyAuth (OpenAPI).
- OpenAI: create a chat completion
  - curl -X POST https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -d '{ "model": "gpt-4o-mini", "messages": [ { "role": "user", "content": "Hello!" } ] }'
  - Docs: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/chat
- OpenAI: upload a file (for use with models; not a repo)
  - curl -X POST https://api.openai.com/v1/files \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY" \
    -F "purpose=assistants" \
    -F "file=@/path/to/file.jsonl"
  - Docs: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/files

Answering your specific questions directly
- Do they handle authentication differently?
  - Yes. GitHub supports app‑based JWT auth leading to short‑lived installation tokens (1‑hour expiry) that can be scoped to up to 500 repos and granular permissions (docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/apps/apps#create-an-installation-access-token-for-an-app). OpenAI uses a straightforward API key (Authorization: Bearer) for all REST calls (docs: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/authentication).
- Do they cover the same kinds of operations for repositories?
  - No. GitHub’s API includes full repository operations: create, list (users/orgs), delete, collaborators, branches, contents, commits, branch protection, and more (docs for each endpoint above). OpenAI’s API has no repository CRUD; its “Files” and “Vector stores” are for AI workflows, not version control or repository administration (docs: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/files and https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/vector-stores).
- Documentation quality
  - GitHub’s REST docs are highly specific about parameters and constraints (e.g., per_page max=100; forbidden parameter combinations returning 422 on GET /user/repos; docs: https://docs.github.com/rest/repos/repos#list-repositories-for-the-authenticated-user) and provide detailed webhook schemas and security headers (https://docs.github.com/webhooks/webhook-events-and-payloads#branch_protection_configuration).
  - OpenAI’s docs clearly organize endpoints by capability, declare ApiKeyAuth, list webhook and streaming event types, and provide endpoint reference pages per feature area (e.g., Responses, Chat, Files, Vector stores, Assistants). Start from https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference.

